        <head>McDOUGALL'S Pateqt SELF—RAISING FLOUR ,),MSTINEIi.). The Examiner, Saturday, February 17, 1906.</head>
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pateqt SELF—RAISING FLOUR ,),MSTINEIi.). The Examiner, Saturday, February 17, 1906.</p>
        <p>CRUEL TEMPTATION.</p>
        <p>Florence Hodgkinson,</p>
        <p>Author of "TINSEL AND GOLD," Etc., U.!</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>• As he stood and looked upon Helen Jar- dine's unconscious ' form a dread agony raged at Lord Alandale's heart; he knew the truth now, knew that he loved her with all his heart, yet he had to stand by and witness her misery without an attempt to help her, had had to hear her plead with a scoundrel for mercy. Oh it was bitter work! Bendinst down on the green sward he took the icy cold hands in his and chafed them tenderly; he had no smelling salts or restorative; he dared not leave Helen alone while he went in search of any, but he lifted the slender form in his strong arms and carried her beyond the shelter of the cedar tree to where the cool night air would blow refreshingly on her face. And it did its work; very soon Helen stirred slightly, then with a bitter gasping sob she came back to life, to the world she found so cruel. " Where am I ? " she asked faintly ; " what has happened ? " " You are quite safe, you are with me," answered Kenneth y "You fainted and I found you under the cedar, tree." "You found me—then you saw—you know—" and she burst into a tit of weeping. Still supporting her with one arm, Lord Alandale tried his best tolteassure her; never had his voice been so tender, his manlier so protecting. "I know nothing," he - said quietly. "I saw a stranger meet you and I gathered that he was no fit person to be a friend of yours, Helen ; you must trust to me to deal with him, but we will not talk of that now; the first thing to think of is to get home." "You will not tell Aunt Mary ? " she pleaded. "I think. I should die of shame. Kenneth, if she knew:"</p>
        <p>It was the first time she had ever called him " Kenneth,' and the sound of his name from her lips thrilled him through and throng. He thought a moment desgerately, racking his brain for the best plait, then an .inspiration dawned on him.</p>
        <p>"When my uncle was alive he used the garden-room for his business sanctum in those days. I had a key of it that I might go in to him direct without passing through the hall and corridor ; that key is still in my possession. I can let you into the garden-room if you can find your way upstairs in the dark ; it is past twelve and the household will have retired. To summon them to admit you would be— undesirable."</p>
        <p>"I can manage easily—there is. a side staircase close to the garden-room."</p>
        <p>"Then we will sta-rt at once. Helen, will you promise meone thing: do not attempt to meet this man again until you and I have had a conversation."</p>
        <p>She gave the promise readily. She knew that Dick Norton would not expect to see her. She had given him every shilling she had in the world, even a most valuable ornament had been yielded to his demands; he only wanted her money or her valuables ; he would not want her to meet him empty-handed.. " I will come and see you to-morrow," Kenneth told her gently, ' to-day, rather, since it is past midnight, and, Helen, try to believe that I will help you to my utmost if only you will trust me." The garden-room was at one end of the long .rambling house. Besides three windows facing the front, it had, at the side, a door opening directly to the grounds, a door which for years had only been used by Kenneth himself ; the key was small since the lock was one of Chubb's patent, and partly from habit Lord Alandale had never taken it from off his watch chain; it stood him in good stead now for he opened the door without the least difficulty and locked it again when Helen was safely within. It cost him something to leave .her to make her way alone through the silent .house to her own room.</p>
        <p>There was little aleep for Lord Alandala that night ;•try as he would he could think ot nothing ;but his beautiful coutini and her troubles, and the only comfort for him_ iva that he would have staked his oath after hearing Helen's appeal to Norton that the man ,was not hit. lover. • What was the link between them ?- What power did the scoundrel hold .which forced liéi to meet him by night and to strip herself ot• all her valuables for his benefit ; for the life ot him Lord Alandale.could not guess. He would have staked his honour that the secret was not discreditable to Helen. that she was sinned against, not sinning, victim rather than criminal, but how she had drifted into her present terror of the stranger he. could :lot guess. It was possible, .of course, that Aubrey Jardine, a weak, easily led man, had com- mitted some offence, against the law of whieh Norton was aware, and that the scoundrel threatened to expose her father's sin if Helen (lid not pay his friend. There was •anothcr theory yet more unwelcome to Lord Alanciale. From the first .days of Helen's aiming to York- shire her aunt had noticed that she shrank with positive aversion from all mention of love or marriage. Was it -possible that in the last weeks of her father's life the two had been so poor she eouldtnot even find the food and comforts his state demanded, and had had to mortgage' herself to Dick Norton. It might be that tile man had supplied money for Mr. .jardine's necessities on the condition that Helen should pledge herself later to •marry him. He might have been away. from Mel- bourne for a time and only returned to find his fiancee flown and a rumour that she had gone to England to be a great heiress. On the whole, painful as the idea was to him, Lord Alandale thought this theory most likely to be the right one. In any case he must see Helen soon, and see her alone. The Matter must rest between themselves.. He would not let Lady Mary have a suspicion of it ; good and kind though she, was, she had very strict illeas upon the behaviour of young girls, an he felt she would judge Helen harshly. He was dawdling with little appetite over a ate breakfast some hours after leaving Helen when a note was brought him from Alandale Hall. The knowledge he was keeping a secret from Lady Mary made him strangely uneasy as to what might be in her letter; but he need not have feared, she only wrote to tell him' she would not be at home to lunch as a letter re- ceived that morning had decided her to go to Yorkifor the day. Some friends of hers were breaking their journey to London by spending two nights in thj cathedral city and she wanted to take the opportunity of seeing them. • Helen would not accompany her, as she thought the railway journey in the heat too much for her. There was no answer to the note the messenger had said, and Kenneth knew it had been written solely to prevent his dropping in to lunch. Lady Mary, in her old world notions of propriety. •would not have thought it correct for two young people to have that meal together unchaperoned. " Aunt Mary can't be home before five or six in the afternoon. go up early and have a talk with Helen."</p>
        <p>It was barely three when he presented him- self at the Hall. It was hard to meet Helen casually as though nothing strange had hap- pened since they were last in that drawing. room together, but under the Argus eyes of the butler Kenneth managed the task.. Only when the man had retreated and closed the door on them the Earl crossed to Helen's side and sat down by her on the sofas !: When I herd Aunt Mary would be out</p>
        <p>for most of the day 1 thougnt I wont(' come up and see you—about last right." She answered nothing, but she looked at him with a piteous entreaty in her beautiful eyes. He felt a monster of cruelty and yet for her sake he had to persist.</p>
        <p>"You mustn't think me meddlesome," he went on. "But you are very young, you have no father or brother to protect you, atid so you must let me step into the breach." "I know yon must despise me utterly," said poor Helen. "I feel ready to sink into the ground with shame. I can't explain things to you, Lord Alandale, I can only tell you this, you can't think of me more harshly than I do myself, and—there's just one thing in the world I long for, to die and be at rest." "Hush 1 ' You must never say that again. If you have made a mistake, Helen, it is not a crime. Oh why won't you trust me and let me do my best to put this miserable tangle right." She shook her head.</p>
        <p>"Because it is impossible." "You will at least let me ask you one ques- tion. Is the man I saw you with last night your lover . . . would-you marry him if you were of age and free to please yourself? " "He is not my 'lover," she cried passion- ately.. "I have known for a long time no , that he was unworthy a woman's faith 'or trust. My only feelings for him are fear and loathing. I have but one desire, never to see his face again." "I think I begin to .understand," said Anlan dale; "he is preying on you on account of some old promise made to him long ago. He wants to force you to marry him against your will."</p>
        <p>Helen shivered, but she neither admitted nor denied the assertion.</p>
        <p>"He wants money," she said bitterly ; "he threatens if I do not give ,it him he. will gc straight to Aunt Mary with his story, and I know she would turn from me in horror. Her favourite virtue is truth. She would have no pity on a girl who'had deceived her." Quite unintentionally these •words utterly misled Lord Alandale. He thought that Helen meant Mr. Norton would tell Lady Mary her niece was engaged to him, and that Helen felt her aunt would condemn her for wanting to break her troth. The girl's real meaning, of course, wts that Norton threat- ened to expose her iraud, -when Lady Mary would certainly have no pity on • the :impostor, who had robbed both her nieces, stealing the name and place of the dead heiress, and de- frauding Magdalen of the inheritance which was hers by right as soon as the sea dosed over Helen. Taking the first view, lienneth had to admit that lus.cousin was right ; Lady Mary would certainly not wish Helen to marry such a person as Dick Norton, but she would never forgive the girl for her double deceptton. beep- ing the .fact of her engagement secret. And assuring her aunt (as she had done again and again) that :love and marriag here we're to alike distasteful. I "I think Ave mast leave Aunt Mary out of this," Lord Alandale said slowly. "To my mind there is only one thing to be done. I will see Mr.. Norton and tell him that you cannot meet him again, that whatever claim he has on you was given when you were- a minor and -is worthless in a court of law, but that since your name must not be lightly slandered by his evil tongue, I, aslkrour nearest kinsman, will pay hifii a hundred pounds on condition of his giving .up all' pretensions to your hand." " A hundred pounds-.-I have not so many shillings." _ _ . ' " "</p>
        <p>"But I have;" Kenneth. answered, "and if you are so very particular you shall repay mc when you come of age some six mouths hence.' Helen hesitated for a. moment while she re- viewed things in her mind. She felt tolerably •certain that in spite of his threats Dick tould not reveal her secret to the Earl, since to do so would cut off all hope of further supplies. • It would be an -inexpressible comfort if he tool: Kenneth's bribe and left • her in peace for the six months until she came of age... She had tong • since decided that before her birth*.: she would lertve Mandale for ever, and cause a notice of her death to be •sent to Lady Mary. Magdalen ,would have {ler rights then and the pretender's crime be blotted out. It was a .terrible -tern ptatiOn. Six months more in the beautiful ,home she had learned tu love so well, six months Of close dailx inter- course with the man who had become her king and hero, no wonder the girl's 'achinn, heart was not proof against it. Lord Mandate left with her authority to go to Mr. -Norton, am' make the best terms he couEL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER IN.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IN.</p>
        <p>MARTHA MARTIN thought the world must be coming to an end when. Lord :Mondale pre- seated himself at Ivy Cottage and told lid he had cowe to see her lodger. . "•Norton's indoors, my lord," said the old servant," but, indeed, he's such an, one voted better not demean yourself by speaking -.! to him.. 'I gave him notice this morning—Dobbs came in and heard me do it just as you advised —and he answered me with such awful oaths and curses 1 had to stuff my fingers in my ears to keep the found out. He says Miss Jill-dine will turn me out of the cottage if I don: to •let the notice stand." _ ,</p>
        <p>" Miss Jardine. will do nothing of the sort." replied - Lord Alandale sternly. " Martha, don't you understand Avliy I want to see Nor- ton ? I won't have in cousin's aame u3ed in this scandalous fashion. I'll make the scoun- drel understand once for all that he most leave. off boasting of his acquaintance with Miss Jardine.", ~• Martha yielded the point and showed ford Alandale into her front parlour. In the dark after days which ,followed all too quickly the poor woman reproached herself bitterly for having, so to say, suffered the interview, but at the same time she knew that no power of hers would, have turned the Earl irom his purpose. They stood facing each other, the high- bred English gentleman, the worthy descen- dant of a icing line of titled ancestry, ancl the man whd borfi in his face the impruss of his worthless. career; even well dressed and groomed, Dick Norton looked what he was, a scoundrel. It made Kenneth's blood bail to. think that his cousin, beautiful Helen , Jar- dine. could ever have fancied she cared lor this miserable ne'er-do-well. " I am Lord Alandale," said the Earl sternly, " and until my cousin, Miss Jardine, comes of age all control over her estate is sested in me. You have told Mrs. Martin that if she persists in your vacating her 'rooms ate will be turned out of this cce.tage. It I.; an abominable lie, as is your assertion that you are an intimate friend of Miss Jardine's.'i 'lf I'm not a friend, I'm something more," said Norton insolently ; " you can ask her if you doubt rue." " I have asked her," said ford Alandale. " I saw you with my cousin last night. I heard your attempt to. blackmail her. 1 .ara the head of her family, and I tell you that I will not allow this persecution of Mss. Jardine to go on." Norton reviewed the situation privately. He would have given Helen up to justice and exposed her fraud without a scruple. but if he</p>
        <p>did so he destroyed all chance of pechniary help for himself. The imposture once known, she would be absolutely penniless. It was better surely to temporise and keep the secret. Six months hence when she was declared of age, and he could make his own terms with her; he would reap a rich reward for his silence now.</p>
        <p>"If you ask Miss Jardine what claim I have on leer, he began craftily," and she answered you the truth, you'll know that so far from persecuting her, I'm the man to whom she trusted herself and her happiness." . . . . .</p>
        <p>Kenneth writhed at tlie. truth concealed in these words, but he went on with icy calm: "I gathered from my cousin that long ago before she was of an age to judge rightly in such matters she promised to be your wife?" "True enough," said Norton; "she cer- tainly did."</p>
        <p>"And I 'understand that hearing she had become a great heiress you have followed her to England with the idea of forcing her to keep her rash promise ? " "You've put it very neatly," said Mr. Norton. "And keep it she shall. I'm a poor man now, but I was much richer than she was when she promised to marry mt, and I don't see that because she's come in for a pot of money and I've lost the little I had she should play fast and loose with me. I told •her straight and plain that if she didn't supply me with funds I should go to Lady Mary' Jar- dine with my story, and so I shall." " Perhaps you don't know the English law, Mr. Norton—l believe your name is Norton." Dick might havoe answered he ought. to know the law by this time, since he had twice suffered imprisonment for breaking it, but he only said surlily: "I don't see what law's got to do with me and Helen. I suppose there's no law to pre- vent a rich girl keeping her promise to a poor man."</p>
        <p>"There are two points of law which have a great deal to do with you, Mr. Norton," said the Earl quietly. "First, any promise made by a minor to her own detriment is not bind- ing; as my cousin is only twenty years of age there can be no question that she was a minor at the time she gave you the alleged promise. Next your threats to her amount to attempts to obtain money' by blackmail; the pun- ishment for that is penal servitude."</p>
        <p>Dick said nothing; he had long since learned the lesson that in some cases the less said the better.</p>
        <p>" You have not the slightest valid claim on</p>
        <p>the conversation nun Deer&amp; in. quiet, ordlnar:- tones. Dobbs had not caught a word of it distinctly, but when in his passion ,the Earl raised his voice Dobbs heard as well as though he had been in the same room.</p>
        <p>"I'll kill you first with my own hands. It would be no crime to rid the world of such as you." Dobbs did not like what he had overheard; the threat rang in his ears with unpleasant persistency. He watched Lord Alandale walk down the street till he was out of rikht, then he remembered some neighbourly errand next door and went round by the 'back way to Mrs, Martin's kitchen. "My, what's the matter ? " he demanled briefly, for the poor woman was sittinr, in her rocking chair with her apron over her head crying as though her heart would break. _</p>
        <p>-" *hat's the. matter, Goody? " he repeated "Is the lodger up to any mischief?" "I only wish I had never set eyes on him," groaned Mrs. Martin. "I'm hit to death, Dobbs, frit to death; there's no other word for it."</p>
        <p>And though he asked no more questions and Mrs. Martin added no further explanation, Police Constable Dobbs knew perfectly well what she was afraid of. She had heard Lord Alandale's threat and dreaded lest harm should come of it.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
