      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ESTABLISH</head>
        <p>ESTABLISH</p>
        <p>J ardine,- resumed, zAh. kinsman I cannot have her subjected to the annoyance of your persecutions, and as the head of her family, I object to any gossip fall- : ing on her. name. To /secure my cousin's peace lam willing to give you.a sum of money flown no condition that you leave this neigh- ; bourhood at once and undertake to hold 113 I further coMmunication with Miss Jardine.", "And how much might you suggest? It ought to be a good gun; since I'm giving up being Prince Consort at Alandale Hall, to say nothing of the pleasure of a beautiful wife."</p>
        <p>Lord Alandale writhed. - .7 I will give you a hundred pounds in gold," he said coldly, ' but I shall, of course, expect you to sign a statement setting forth that you relinquish all claim to Miss Jardine's hand.', " A hundred pounds is precious little ; you had better make it live."</p>
        <p>"Not a penny more than one hundred." " I: can't decide suddenly like: this," said Nerton in a whining tone ; "you must give me a day or two to think over it. This is Wednesday; on Saturday, if you like to call, you shall have my answer." " I shall' not call again," said Lord Alan- dale. "If you write and tell me you accept My terms, the money shall be ready for you ; but, mark my words, even if you refuse them, I can, and will, protect Miss Jardine' from your cowardly attacks. - You will have to leave this house next Wednesday. You Will find no other lodgings open to you. If N'oll attempt to take up your quarters at the village inn, Miss Jardine will be sent abroad to avoid your molestations." " Hang it all," cried Norton, "I am Ihe girl's own chbice and I've every right to her, and her fortune, tod." " You shall never have either," replied Lord Alandale. Then the mocking laugh on the other's lips roused him to passionate anger; and forgetting all need for caution 'he cried loudly, "Aneif there was no other way of preventing it I'd kill you with my own hands. It would be no crime to rid the world of such as you." He turned and went out. Martha stood in the narrow passage' through which he had to pass, her face white with terror, but it never occurred to Kenneth that she had heard his words and believed they meant a threat to murder her unwelcome lodger. Dobbs, the constable, lived next door to Ivy Cottage. The partition wall between the -two houses was thin enough for him to hear Lord Alandale's Darting threat. The first part of</p>
