        <p>31 YEARS.</p>
        <p>much to bring about a better feeling between the two peoples. The idea was laid down that oppression could only come about by a reactionary military government and not from the nation. And the industrial classes in a large degree needed an army for protecting external markets and privileges in colonies, it was clear that upon the shoulders of the proletariat lay the burden of military expenses. Under present conditions he had no interest in his own country. On the contrary, his interest consisted in enlarging and cheapening home markets, which was at variance with the protected capitalists whose aim was to cheapen production and sell at a good price, particularly abroad. For example, the dumping of German goods into England could not exist if the wages in Germany were higher and German people not compelled to buy German goods. That explained the international idea of the proletariats' tight for closer intercourse between nations which were opposed to the nationalistic feeling of industrial expansion. Therefore, they were desirous of closer Union with the Itussiarr proletariat's in spite of the majority of Polish people being against them. They wanted higher wages for workmen, but the menu- - fn.:starer had said that it would ruin ; industry. The fight of Socialism with manufacturers and ULTRA-VIOLET RAYS AND GLASS. Government was the main social problem in The action of ultra-vioret rap; upon- glass has Poland. It was no extraordinary thing for been observed by Franz Fischer, a German a boy of twelve to read sdme revolutionary paper to his comrade, while they were secretly engaged in read- scientist. In order to make the researches, he mercury arc contained in a quartz tube as lug Poli history and literature. Through that means, the natin grew revolutionary ideas. Notwithstanding sli au.sesos source of e are placed quite near the tube, separated from of the rays. Samples of different kinds the daily xecutions of Socialists, the fight' still pro- 21 ‘la it