      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SOCIAL CONDITIONS IN POLAND.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL CONDITIONS IN POLAND.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Junior Liberal</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Tennuebaum, of Warsaw, presented an Interesting paper on social conditions in Poland at the meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association which was held at Gregory's Cafe on Monday. The meeting was well attended and was under the chair- manship of Mr. E. Osborne, president of the Associa- tion.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. H. GRAY (joint hon. secretary) read the paper on behalf of Mr. Tennuebaum. Having briefly reviewed the annexation of Poland by Russian, tboovriter stated thatprior to 1795,the year of the war with Russia Poland had been ilargely ['controlled by landowners and the peasants, as elsewhere in Europe, were in a state of slavery. Industry could not develop itself. The capitalists, artizans and peasants were deprived of political power. Poverty adoubded on all sides except in the houses of the, landowners. Similar conditions existed in Prussia, Austria, and Russia. Those countries began to develop the imperialistic idea. On account of the feebleness of the people in Poland—this feeble- ness being brought about by their absence of political power —no centralization of government could occur. That brought about anarchy. The Poles were eeprived of self government by the Russians the country being placed under the complete ,control of Russian autocracy. In 1863 a new epoch opened in the annals of Polish industry. The abolition of tariff boundaries between Russia and Poland proved an important stimulus to trade and industrial develop- ment with its attendant advantages an' disadvantages. was very rapid. The social inequalities became the vexed problem of the hour as on the one hand the workers were in a state of great poverty whilst the capitalists made huge dividends. The more intelligent workman associated themselves with the socialist movement while the tyranical capitalist looked to the Government for support both in the oppressing of the workers and in the raising of tariff woes against foreign goods, and with this object the more wealthy bad obtained powerful positions in Government circles. In face of these conditions social reform hed made little progress. As regarded education, ia the elementary and secondary schools, and also in the Universities of Poland, the Russian Government com- pelled the staff- to write and speak in Russian. That gave great sorrow to the Polish people, and the children were taught in their early , years to look towards the Russian Government as their greatest foe. In their postal system, they could not register a letterin Polish, and those things offended the most deep and sacred feel- ings of every Pole. Great difficulties were caused through a Pole having to speak Russiamin a Polish public place, which meant violent opposition from his fellows. It was not until the last year or two that the majority of Russian people turned round upon their Government. The recent revolution in Russia had done</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>iginal</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
