      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>rid Famo</head>
        <p>rid Famo</p>
        <p>UNG TONIC</p>
        <p>• whrla</p>
        <p>REFUSE IMITAT</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>CLIMATE OF THE ARCTIC REGIONS.</p>
        <p>According to the usual theories of glaciation the climate of Arctic regions would be more severe in the glacial period than it , is at present in the same proportion as the climate of this country was then colder than it is now. Dr. Maddren, however, who has been exploring Alaska in search of fossil mammoth, concludes from his observations that the climates of Arctic and sub-Arctic regions Were never colder than they are at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SEX AND PERCEPTION.</head>
        <p>SEX AND PERCEPTION.</p>
        <p>In the Psychological Review an aecoutft has been given of an investigation carried out by Mabel S. Nelson as to the , difference between men, and women in the matter of the recognition of colour • and the perception of sound. The conclusion is that men are clearly superior in the recognition of blue, and women possibly in the matter of yellow. As regards hearing, the • •e- -suit is that men hear better 'than women, whit* both can hear farther with the right eat; than with the left.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOW DOES THE GECKO CLING?</head>
        <p>HOW DOES THE GECKO CLING?</p>
        <p>Does the Gecko cling by electrical action? It has been generally supposed that, like the house- fly on the ceiling, this little lizard did lo by means of suctorial discs on the extremities of its toes. According, to Dr. Schmidt, however, in the Jena Zeitschrift, this is not so. The gecko lies on the under surface of its toes scales provided with clusters of fine hair-like bodies ending in minute flat surfaces, instead of points. By means of blood spaces, these, are capable of acting ,as erectile organs. In clinging to ,an under-surface the gecko is 'supposed to hold on by these hair- like bodies. And it is considered to be not im- probable that the actual power is the electricity supplied by these structures.</p>
        <p>WHEN AFRICA AND AMERICA JOINED. Palteontologists have for some time peen con- vinced that the fossil forms found in South America, South Afnca, and Australia indicate a comparatively recent land connection between those countries. Fresh evidence for this opinion has recently been brought forward. A fossil- insectivorous mammal - found at Santa Cruz, Patagonia, is closely related to a form found in South Africa, while a carnivorous mar- supial found in the same region is closely akin to an Australian form. This land connection may, it is thought, have existed at the close of the Cretaceous, or beginning of the Tertiary period. Over it the animals of one country may have passed to the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>STAR-STREAMING.</head>
        <p>STAR-STREAMING.</p>
        <p>It is often very difficult for the layman to grasp, .says Engineering, how astronomers can fix the positions of celestial bodies with that wonderful accuracy of which every eclipse an every occultation gives fresh evidence. The earth turns about its axis, but that axis vacil- lates, though very slightly indeed, yet per- ceptibly. 'By the time the earth has completed its yearly orbit around the sun, the sun has swept milli of miles in sits course through space, apparently towards a Lyrae. Various methods allow us to trace the character of this motion. Star-groups which we are approaching seem to open out; those which we leave beind seem, to clbse up. When the constant of the pre- cessidn of the equinoxes is to be derived, or the motion of the sun through space is being in- vestigated, it is usually assumed that this appa- rent proper motion of the various stars follows no general law. Professor Kapteyn, of Groe- ningen, however, who has made a Study of this difficult problem, has arrived at the conclusion that we have in the universe to deal with two star-streams moving in opposite directions. Pro- fessor Kapteyn has been able to determine -the real points towards which the star streams ap- pear to be directed. He calls these points the vertices, and. declares that the conditions of his .problems are such 'that the vertices of stellar motion must he diametrically opposite to one another. Professor Kapteyn believes that the stars nearest to the solar system partly partici- pate in the motion of the solar system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>LIGHT OF THE Sl7:</head>
        <p>THE LIGHT OF THE Sl7:</p>
        <p>It sometimes seems that in the contemplation of the sun's radiation our eyes, even with all their perfections, are just as imperfect in inter- preting fully what the sun dispenses; as would be the ears of a man who could only hear tho beating of a drum when attempting to listen to an orchestral performance of a beautiful sym- phony. The solemn tones of the organ, the ex- quisite delicacy of the stringed instruments, would be alike inaudible; all that he could hear would bo the tones of a solitary instrument. Almost equally imperfect, says the "Story of the Sun," are our ejwi for the interpretation of those marvellous radiations, which the sun sheds abroad with such prodigality. His beams really contain a vast keyboard of vibrations of which -Jur eyes can only disclose to us/ a couple ,of octaves. Here and there within the mighty corn- pass of the instrument we are able to devise a photographic plate, or a Hertzian resonator, to astonish us with a few additional octaves, but of tho full volume of the „harmony we are almost entirely ignorant. When we think that the sun pours forth its radiations with such prodigality that our earth is not able to intercept ~the two- thousand-millionth part of the whole; when we think that the other suns in space, to the npm. 'ber of many millions, aro each of them simul- taneously filling the ether with their' orchestra of, vibrations; when we think of the marvellous del licacy with which that medium transmits all these vibration*--we begin to realise the majesty of the words, "Let there be light."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TRADE WINDS</head>
        <p>THE TRADE WINDS</p>
        <p>That the , south-west winds ich are observed on the Peak of Teneriffe are be return trade winds is the accepted statemet,;. of the text books.• Some recent • explorations of tho upper currents of the air, however, by means of kites have seemed 40 throw doubt on this view. As the result of a nuMber of kite flights carried on during a cruise in the Prince of :Monaco's yacht near the Azores and Canaries, Professor Hergesell contends that there is no evidence of the existence of these anti-trade winds in those regions. "A current from the south-west corre- sponding to , the theoretical return trade winds has," he says, "never ,been found by kites. though they have often risen above the heiOlit of the Peak of Teneriffe." Professor Hergesell concludes that the south-west , winds observed on the Peak, and considered to ,be the return trades. are of local origin, and due to the influence of the island. The recompensing current, he eon- tends, flows -in a north-west direction in the iegion investigated by him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>OBSERVATIONS ON THE CURRENTS.</head>
        <p>OBSERVATIONS ON THE CURRENTS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. A. L. Rotch and L. Tiesserence de Burt, however, themselves pioneers in the ex- ploration of the upper air by means of kites, con- sidered that such sweeping conclusions required further investigations. So a series of observa- tions have been made by 'their assistants on board the Otaria. chiefly in the neighbourhood of Madeira, Teneriffe, and the Cape Verde Islands. The conclusions deduced from these observations are that up to heights of from 0.7001 t. to 16,2001 t. the winds blowing towards the Ouator vary be- tween north.east and north-west. the latter usu- ally forming the upper layer. These are the trade winds. Above them are currents blowing south-east, south, and south-west, and forming the anti-trades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>POLO-CYCLISTS PERIL</head>
        <p>A POLO-CYCLISTS PERIL</p>
        <p>How Tom Athol Nearly Lost his Nerve.</p>
        <p>Have you ever witnessed a display of cycle-polo by well-matched teams? Cycle-polo is easentially4 game calling for indomitable pluck and dash, and when a player gets out of condition and loses his nerve he runs considerable risk of losing his life too. IMr. Tom Athol, of the original Anglo-American Bioycle Polo team, was recently interviewed ,by a representative of the "Brighton Standard," to whom ,he told an interesting story, of how he escaped the danger of losing his nerve. "In January last I began to feel out of condition,' said Mr. Athol. "I soon became wretchedly ill and ; weak. Immediately after meals giddiness and horrible sinking feelings would attack me, and I had acute pains, in the chest and side. My nights were passed in misery for ,I was unable to sleep, my nerves were very bad, and I had "ever° heart palpitation. This sort of thing went on for some weeks and made me fearfully depressed. ' All the remedies weeks, proving unavailing. I eventu- ally wrote to my mother informing her of my ill-health and in reply received such a vigorous recommendation of bile beans for billiousness that I immediately purchased a ! box. "The effect was simply wonderful, and I decided at once to continue the medicine. Soon I was eating and' ' sleeping better, the pains in my body gradually pawed away, and I was once more restored to my accustomed youthful spirits and energy. Now lam enjoying the very best of health." If you are out of condition from overwork, domestic anxiety, lose of sleep, liver troubles, indigestion,,con- stipation, or through the inclemeacy of the weather, bile beans, which proved so powerful a restorative in Mr4Athol's case, will restore you to health and enery. ; But be careful that you are not put off with specious imitations. You can get bile beans at one-and-three- half-pence or two-and-nine per box. Steer clear of i cheap substitutes, for there s nothing " just as good as bile beans " for creating robust health and steady , nerves.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH BUILDER PROSECUTED. Mr. T. Williams and the Parochial Committee. An interesting case was held at Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday when Thomas Williams, 34, White- field-road, Stockton Heath, builder, was summoned for erecting a building without sending a notice and com- plete plans as required by the bylaws. Mr. G. F. Ashton, the clerk, appeared foe-the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council and Mr. Steel for the defendant. Mr. Ashton said the proceedings were taken under , by-law 102 which laid down that a person who intended , to erect al building should give notice to the Rural District Council and send complete plans of the build- ing. The building in question was in the rear of the house occupied by Mr. Williams and had been erected by Mr. 'Williams without submitting a plan. The offence in this case was more aggravated as Mr. Williams I was a member of the Parochial Committee and had to I sit with the other members and receive plans from other owners. Mr. Williams had been warned on four or five previous occasions for having broken the Council's bylaws. On most of these occasions, promises had been made that such an offence should not occur again. He bad had plenty of warning, and the Council could not allow the matter to go further, and they brought action in the hope that such a penalty would be inflicted as would prevent defendant and other builders of property from contravening their by-laws in the future. Mr. Edward Hough, deputy clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Council, said that no notice had been received from Mr. Williams to erect a building in White- field-road, There had been four offences of a similar character during the past three years. On April 9th, 1903, the Surveyor reported that the defendant com- menced building in Cawdor-street,Stockton Heath,with- out submitting plans and the Clerk was instructed to call Mr. Williams's attention to the breach of the by- laws. On October let, in the same year, the Surveyor repolted that the defendant had commenced building in Whitefleld-road, Stockton Heath,on land for which plans had been passed for another builder, and the defendant had not submitted any plans. His attention wage/rain - called to the breach of the bylawsf and he was called upon to cease building until be had submitted plans, In 1905, the defendant erected houses instead of shops. He had had plans passed for the erection of shops but , he erected houses in lieu thereof and Mr. Williams wrote expressing regret. On another occasion the defendant commenced building in Cawdor-street with- out submitting plans.—ln answer, to Mr. Steel, witness stated that no notice was given to the defendant of the k breach of this particular bylaw. The contravention on previous occasions had been in respect of new build- m g:. r. Steel: I submit that the building by Mr. , Williams is an alteration to his house. Mr. Hough: Ido not admit that. , Mr. John Swinton, surveyor to the Council, Said the matter came to his notice on December 21st, 1905. He visited the billiard room on January Ist arid found that [it was 23ft. 6in, in length, 16ft, in width and 10ft. ' high. The building appeared to bie used as a billiard ; room. No plans had been sent in to the Council.—ln answer to Alio. Steel witness stated that his attention was called by the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee to the breach of the bylaws. Mr. IVilliams was not on the Sub-building Committee, but plans came before the Parochial Committee. The sub-committee inquired into the details and afterwards the plans went before the Parochial Committee and Mr. Williams had oppor- tunities of seeing them. A great number of outbuild- ings had been erected during recent veers and many had been erected without plans having lieen submitted. Witness was then questioned by Mr. Steel with fegard to certain outbuildings which had been erected. The Surveyor replied to some of the questions that plans had not been submitted because they were not -- necessary. He could not remember whether plans had been submitted for the schoolroom in Walton-road and he did not know i[vhether an oil shed had been erected by Mr. Whitley who was also a member of the Parochial Committee. Mr. Steel: Did Mr. Welsby submit plans for the in his yard ? Witness: ‘No. He apologised for not having done so •</p>
        <p>By Mr. Steel : Can you tell me why Mr. Williams should be singled out for prosecution? —Mr. Salon : Because he has been cautioned so many tin Mr. Ste : The Parochial Committee want to make an example of him ? Witness : That is so. Mr. Williams stated that he had been carrying on busipess for the last six or seven years. He was not a .meniber of the Sub-building Committee, and during the two years he had been a member of the Parochial Com- mittee he had not known a plan to be submitted for the erection of an outbuilding.. He told Mr. Swinton that. be had not the sligbsest idea a plan was necessary. Had he known, it would have been prepared. He was prepared to submit one now. In another case in which he had commenced building without snbmttting plans Mr. Swinto'n had told him it was all right. Mr. Steel said that Mr. Williams had been made a scapegoat. This prosecution had been ordered by defendant's co-members of the Parochial Committee. and this was evidently a matter of spite on their part. Two members of the committee bad infringed the very bye-laws in question themselves—two of the men who had.ordered the prosecution of Mr. Williams. Mr. Welsby erected a shed in his back yard, and Mr. Whitley had built an oil shed, and so far as Nfr. Williams knew no plans had been submitted in either case. Mr. Long said the Bench were agreed that the case must be dismissed on payment of the costs. This seemed only a slight case as compared with others. Mr. Steel : The plan shall be submitted. —— -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>RHEUMATIC —ERUPTIONS. Skin-torture added to Bodily- Pain Zam-Buk Cures.</head>
        <p>RHEUMATIC —ERUPTIONS. Skin-torture added to Bodily- Pain Zam-Buk Cures.</p>
        <p>A very bad form of skin disease, usually caused by !the presence of acid in the perspiration, or the use of crude ernbrocations containing harmful mineral pro- ducts, often atta'alts rheumatic subjects. Rheumatism is particularly prevalent just now, owing to the bad weather, and it is well to note that, beside curing the eruptions, Zavn-Buk is invaluable for the "rheumy pains themselves. Mr. Henry Savage, miner, of 57, Brassey-street„ Alfreton-road, Nottingham, says :—" For about eight years I was on and off my club owing to this sickness. My skin was in a terrible state, particularly about the left leg. It was one mess of sores from knee to ankle, and the agony was fearful. I often had to ride about, and was once laid up four months at a stretch ! I had treatment both privately and at the hospital. The doctor said the sores were due to rheumatism in the bone, and gave me some lotions to use. These did not appear to give me relief, however, and I resorted to Zam-Iluk. The doctor appeared sceptical, but when I told him I was actually feeling better, be said : IVell, go on with it if you like.' I persevered with Zarn-Rulc, and, although I had just been ten weeks off work while under other treatment, I was fit to start again within a fortnight after beginning to use Zam-Buk. Zarn-Billi did more for me than the hospital and doctors put together. I can now get a quiet night's rest as a regular thing, my skin is healthy, the irritation has disappeared, and I can walk about quite comfortably." Zam-lluk cures rheumatic eruptions, rashes, chronic sores, scabbing and scaling skin, eczema, vlcers, bad legs, diseased ankles, sore backs, poisoned or festering sores, piles, scalp irritation, chapped hands, chilblains. chills, abscesses, boils, pimples, ringworm, running sores, stiffness, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, scratches, sprains, rheumatism, and all aches and pains. All chemists sell Zam-Buk in Is. 14d. and 2s. 9d. boxes (2s. 3d. size contains nearly four times as much as the Is. Ed.) ; or it may be obtained post free from the Zam:Buk Co., Red Cross Street, London, KC,</p>
      </div>
