      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Late Countess Howe.—The sad death of Countess Howe, who enjoyed the intimate friendship of the Ring and Queen,places a large number of noble families in mourning. The wife of Earl Howe—Charaterlain to Queen Alexandra—she was one of the large family of the seventh Duke of Marlborough, and of her five sisters all are living save one—the Into Lady Tweed- mouth—namely Lady Winborne, Lady de Vernsey, the Howager Dimbeep of Roxburghe and Lady Sarah Wilson. Lady Howe hatrin a large measure the oratorial gifts - of her distinguished brother Lord Randolph Churchill, and was often seen on the public platform. She was as active on the Conservative side as Lady Tweedmouth was on behalf of the Liberals, to whom also Lady Wimborne gives her assistance. In the field, Lady Howe was noted for her dash and daring, and was a famous Whip. Some three or four years ago the Countess had a bad attack of neuritis, and the King afterwards placed the royal yacht ' Osborne," at her disposal in the hope that she would benefit from tbe sea air. She however never really recovered, and for the pest two or three seasons was only able to attead social entertainments in a bath chair. Lady Howe was born in 1860 and leaves an only son Viscount Curzon, j who attained his majority last year. Copenhagan.—After Paris,there is no city where mot objects of art abound than in that gay little capital— Copenhagan—wbich at the moment is sorrowing most truly and deeply, the loss of Ring Christian IX. There are very excellent shops in Copenhagen, many of them tucked away in all sorts of odd corners, and the flower parket is one of the prettiest sights in the world. quite unattended members of the Royal Family walk about the streets, such a thing as being mobbed or stared at, being quite unknown to them. . Half-Mourning Colors.—What with the Conn Mourn- Ing and Lent fast approaching, half-mourning colours as.t: almost tho only ones in evidence. These include IP" ahaile of violet, all the varied loveliness of the fte hio and Li ssom, and the scented heliotrope, while greys, quest.ae.„lt,and white mixtures, are in the greatest re- heat i item is an old raying that women look their ; Uneo4a skilful admixture of black and white, and 1 heliej. 43(117 there is much of truth in this ancient iNew nraids.—Braid is in high favour in tailor lard, at "`l° Patterns deemed mos. desirable are those which distance afford the appearance of coarse lace. On fl.g.at'oolonred gowns, white and gold braids, with the eotntest touch of colour are having an immense vogue. 44,150 are the gold and silver tinsel braids, finished feather" edge. Braids in one colour, and less i;tPeiful in design, are more useful however, and less eIY to mark a costume that has to be worn for more .. an one season. oVhow Sleeves.—Tt is a matter for regret that the v.! ow sleeve is still to the fore, among tailors. On ._" score of grace, the idea has positively nothing to .7uernmend it, and as it renders long gloves a stern fecessity. nothing in the was' of economy can be said 'c'tir.it. The elbow sleeve for cloth walking gowns is !:1 incongruity, and the sooner tailors recognise this tact the better for those who leave the que-tion of Style in their hands. Even when elaborate eternoon &amp;won,: of voile, cashmere, or taffeta are concerned, the Possess sic" is usaelly a mistake. Few women _. really good • Imo a tranenarency ''' I tils'esPPela Y ' 11 from elbow to wrist, Renovations.—Ths in better taste. favour,which will b.: "pinafore " blouse has returned to Yoke and sleeves are a contrasting colour to Usuallyiweioome news to the.ronovator.the ' completes the lc, whichC faobfrlace or silk and lace, of _blouse, which of course must match that of the skirt. \ This is gathered oa to the yoke and the shoulder straps, Which are cut in one with it, fasten—apparently—on , top, with one large button. Those among us who Possess blouses with hopeless sleeves, and no material to match, wherewith to fashion others, will do well to consider the merits of the" pinafore" blouse. ~, Snuff-Taking.—We seem likely to be spared the . crinoline sense" this season, hilt another one comes !rat.n Paris, to the effect that fashionable women are ,a_king snuffing. In the old times, the elegant manner ..13,r taking snuff was regarded almost as an art, in which ,` seems the fair Nrisienne ii.exelediagly graceful and „ccomplished. But for all that, and also the fact that Renff-boxes, cigarette dases,and match boxes, commonly evg,;ire among the prizes offered at women's card parties, e " nwl not be afraid that fashionable soeiety will take i't'ltionsly to the dirty habit of taking saaff.As a pretence te,,l")i,glat be tolerated, hut if it is not mere affectation a • andkerchief would he required one for which the e• nable woman has no receptr 31e, of which, if it ,he stowed away, could hardly be produced in the .rigoo of others. , thoua Dinapnearing " At Home" Day.—ln America of tillocial changes which have dispensed with many fttorA° old-fashioned manners and custom., are even 4 aft,- marked than is the case in this country. The ancll oon is no longer spent in going fromone house to tria,;•_ret Diving duty calls, or leaving cards. The 41,11"" o Society woman has discovered that there are Bei 3' ways of spending an afternoon more pleasantly a 11 that, and as everything is now sacrificed to getting .4..8 much out of life as possible, the regular " at home P'Y has come to be regarded as a waste of time, and is eist becoming a raattmr of ancient history in New York ancl also in London. Women call on their friends when they have occr-ion to, and at odd times, and perhaps mice or twice in the season pay off their social debts ,n, masse by giving an afternoon reception and enter- tainment at one of the hotels or restaurants. ~ The " Best Parlour."—Dr. Somerville Hastings, in a ,ecture at the Institute of Hygiene, was rather severe tiPon the practice of the Ilritish housewife, of devoting a room— generally the best in the house—to the recep- tion of occasional visitors. No doubt a great many People do err in this respect, giving up an aparttnent 'which they can ill spare to use on rare occasions, while the family spend the greater part of tbeir leisure in another which is not nearly so comfortable or con- venient. In the case of many working people, the family it in the kitchen, while the best room is only brought :lino use on Sundays. But the housewives would per- !laps say that they derive the greatest amount of satis- Laction from the method which they adopt, and Dr. tlastin.'s would experience some difficulty in replying to that statement New Books.—Last week Messrs. Cassell and Co., pub- lished another n, r of Morice Gerard—the anther of 4` k Lieutenant of the King," viz., "The Red Seal " ;</p>
