        <p>Ia story full of exiting incidents, and with an absorbirg love interests which add to its charm. This week Messrs ICassell are issuing "The Mystery of the Shadow," by Fergus Hurne,the author of The Mystery of a Hansom Cab,' and nest week this firm will publish " The Burglars' Club, a romance in Twelve Chronicles," by Henry A. Hering. MADGE.</p>
        <p>LUMBAGO LOST. Once an aching sufferer, Incapable of work and hopeless of relief. Strength and Acthbity were restored by</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Piiis.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Patterson, who lives at 57, Ha nrsh-st re et, Newcastle-on-Tyne, fought in the Soudan and South African Wars. Returning home he accepted a positior atElswick Ordnance Works. Hestates</p>
        <p>"One day I caught a severe cold. It seem break me up completely, and from tha continually from chills and fever. I could not get warm, and every bone in my body ached. Excruciating pains shot across my kidneys and the small of my hack. causing many sleepless rights." " Becoming alarmed. I consulted a medical man, who pronounced my case to be Lumbago. I took physic but I did not feel better—in fact, as time passed, I grew worse and was no longer, capable of doing work% properly." " All vigour and activ- ity bad left me. My appetite fell away, and Mr. Wm. _ I suffered from acute indigestion. The pains Cared of Lumbago and Loss of Strength by in my cls,!st and sides Dr. wiiiiams, pink pinks grew snore yinjent, until I was repelled by the sight of food.</p>
