      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>, The Exarainer, Saturday, February 17, 1906. WOMAN'S REALM:</head>
        <p>, The Exarainer, Saturday, February 17, 1906. WOMAN'S REALM:</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>BUTTON NOVELTIES.</p>
        <p>Button novelties are so many and so various that it is difficult to keep pace with the lead- ing designs. An innovation has lately appeared in the form of clever imitations of American Indian beadwork, while hand-painted porcelain and mother -o' -pearl buttons are equally in favour with the modern elegante. Coloured imitation jewels are likewise introduced on the new buttons employed by the leading couturieres, but these are used only sparingly, not more than one or two being countenanced on a gown or wrap. THE CRAZE FOR WHITE.</p>
        <p>The smartest frock in the world looks ridicu- lous when worn out of its proper setting, which was forcibly brought home to my mind, says a writer in the Onlooker, by some very glaborate and exquisitely-cut white coats and skills I met recently at Church Parade at Brighton. It was blowing a regular north-east gale, and anything more unbecoming to wllite frocks than blue lips and red noses it would be hard to imagine. Why —oh, why cannot women dress suitably? That gale called for a warm tone of cerise, or vivid crimson, or even navy blue or brown, but white —unredeemed by sables—was downright unkind. The craze for white this winter is really rather trying; one meets cropped rabbit or white cat masquerading "as ermine" at every corner. Why not own up toiobit. and do away with the spiky " paint-brus ' that take the place of the orthodox " tails "?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ZOIJAVE COATEE.</head>
        <p>ZOIJAVE COATEE.</p>
        <p>Corselet skirts are now an acknowledged mode, and a very fascinating one into the bar- gain, for the trim rounded appearance they give to the waist is very pleasing, and it certainly comes as a relief after the everlasting swathed belt that has held our affections captive for so long. With these corselet skirts are worn, for the most part, little coatees or blouses arranged with a zouave effect, and one of these zouaves is</p>
        <p>or sketch. It is formed of silk</p>
        <p>-ape cut on the cross, and having the inter- spaces filled in with piece lace. The zouave is inished with a collar and front of tucked silk,</p>
        <p>Le same silk being employed in the rosettes iat decorate the little front. The sleeves have tucked cuff reaching to_ the elbow, where they id in a full little flare. Other materials, such velvet or satin, might take the place of the in this dainty little corselet zouave.</p>
