      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Eliza Monahan's funeral at Rosehill, Chicago, was attended by women only. A woman said the prayers, a woman spoke the words of eulogy over the body, and other women consigned the coffin to the grave.</p>
        <p>• A woman who asked the Lambeth Guardians for outdoor relief said she was employed by a big firm of contractors, receiving ncl. for finishing a pair of Volunteer's trousers. The most she could earn was lid. an hour, and she had to provide her own silk and thread.</p>
        <p>A curious and most Agnificant sign of the Limos is contained in the following message from a Tokio correspondent: "In connection with the woman's suffrage movement in Japan a Bill is to be introduced in the Diet as the .first step, permitting women to be members of political parties."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SERVICEABLE WALKING COSTUME.</head>
        <p>SERVICEABLE WALKING COSTUME.</p>
        <p>February finds us busily turning over in our minds the replenishments that our wardrobes will require with the first breath of spring. A coat and skirt is, of course, our first thought, for nothing is so likely to disgust us with our pre- sent costumes as a, few bursts of brilliant spring sunshine. Our sketch deals with a smart but plain little walking costume, which will do good service right through the spring months., The</p>
        <p>coat has a plain, full sleeve, ending in a gaunt- let cuff, faced with velvet. The collar is also covered with velvet, but the long tapering revers are quite plain. The coat is open in front, and ends in a. short, full basque. The waistcoat may be of some contrasting material or shade, and should be made detachable, so that it can be exchanged for another or discarded alto- gether on a warm day. The skirt is fully gored, and trimmed with three wide straps. "ALWAYS PICK OFF THE STRAWS."</p>
        <p>One of this most delightful and humorous chapters in Mrs. John Lane's book, "The Cham- pagne Standard," is that on "Temporary Power." Here is a short extract: "Of course, a 'bus is low, but you need not say you came in one, only _you must be careful! The other day old Lady TOPPingham called, and grew quite elo- quent on the levelling influences of 'buses; they might do for cooks and tradespeople, she said, but her principles were such that she really couldn't ride in one. All the time she was clutch- ing a blue punched 'bus ticket on the top of her card-case with her relentless thumb. I agreed with her, and said that I also never could nor would, and no sooner had she gone than I was off to Whiteley's on top of a blue Kensington. Still, it is levelling, and you should always pick off the straws and never cling to the tickets.'</p>
