      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>APPLE MERINGIJE.—Dip 80M0 pieces of stale cake in milk and place in a pie-dish. Take eight good-sized apples, pare and core them, and place In slices over the cake. Sprinkle with eight good tablespoonfuls of sugar, and bake the apples in * moderate oven till tender. Beat the whites of six eggs ana six trupiespoontuis pi castor sugar with a few drops of vanilla flavour to a stiff froth, and heap this over the apples. Let the meringue brown in the oven, and when cold serve with cream.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Popular Penny Concerts. There was again a large attendance at the popular penny concert in the Bold-street Schoolroom on Satur- day evening, when an excellent entertainment was pro- vided. Songs were contributed by Miss A. Woodland, Messrs. E. Walker and J. Clayton, and Master H. Griffiths, and a duet by the Misses Snelling and Leicester, all being well received. Mr. J. Craik also contributed' to the success of the entertainment by playing two violin solos, while Mr. Burdett caused much laughter with his humorous sketches. Mrs. B. Foreman was the accompanist. The concert was arranged by Mr. James Clayton. P.S.A. Meeting. , An" open meeting" of the Warrington Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street on Sun- day and was well attended. Mr. Hickman presided, and 'gave a short address, quoting a saying of John Wesley: " Ntiver be unemployed, and never be use- lessly employed." He urged each one present to find some .useful work to do. A stiking address was delivered by the Rev. Frank Vaughan of Emmanuel Reformed Church. Two solos "The voice in the cathedral "and " Lead, kindly Light," were sung in a very creditable_ manner by Mr. E. Walker. Mr. J. Pownall was the accompanist. Mr. T. Cawley read the Scripture lesson, and Mr. R. Nelson took part in the service. Four new members were enrolled.</p>
        <p>• "'The Times of Queen Elizabeth." At the usual fortnightly meeting of St. John's Literaryland Social Society, held in St. John's school- room on Monday evening, the President, the Rev. J. L. Milne, 8.D.," in the choir, Mr. F. A. Yeomans (Liverpool) read a paper On "The Times of Queen Elizabeth." Remarking that it was only pcssible in the time at their disposal to take a brief glimpqe into those times, Mr. Yeomans described shortly the con- dition of England at the accession of Elizabeth, after- wards dealing in a highly interesting manner with the chief events and features of the reign. There was some discussion of the subject by several of those present, and at the close Mr, Yeomans was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for his paper, which was declared to be mcst instructive and interesting. Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Limps, &amp;c.—J S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Garton's Farm Seeds. Farm seed catalogues are legion at present but the one just issued by the well-known firm of Gartons Limited, of Warrington and Newton-le-Willows, is so original and distinct in character that it calls for specio notice: This list is beatifolly illustrated by reproauctions of the firm's well-known new and improved breeds of oats, barleys, grasses, clovers, Gmtr aei rn talg eo 111,?3 wfahonri cd h theirbtua rynei Ps, as well as a few of the many d recently been awarded to Messrs. famous seeds. Perhaps the most interesting feature of the list is the illustrated article entitled "The botanical analysis of the seeding of a clover plant," which should be read by all those inter- ested in seeds of this kind The principals of this firm, Robert Garton, George P. Miln, and Thomas R. Garton, are well-known in the agricultural world as being thoroughly practical men, and they are to be contratulated upon the production of this interesting work.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett and the General Election. Alderman Bennett, speaking at a meeting of the Executive Council of the Lancashire and Cheshire Contervative Working Men's Federation held at the Manchester Conservative Club on Saturday, said that though the Liberals were claiming the result of the election as a victory for Free Trade, in his opinion it was nothing of the kind. The Liberals had put forth a number of lying statements and placards on Chinrie labour, and other subjects which had been far more daelt on. He moved : "That this Executive Council decrecates and condemns the scandalous misrepresen- tations to which the recent Liberal victory is largely due, and is deeply impressed with the importance of continuing to educate the constituencies in the principles of Tory democracy. It ventures to respect • fully urge en the party leaders the supreme necessity of bringing the central office into closer touch with Unionist feeling, making it really representative, and giving Labour its due voice in its decisions, and suggests a special meeting of the party with this in view. It also reiterates its faith in the policy, of Imperial Union based' on preferential trade, and emphasizes the desirability of continuing to associate with it a broad and generous policy of genuine social reform."—The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Up-to-date Fire Places.-3. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Funeral of the Late Mr. H. B. White. The funeral of the late Mr. Henry Brown White, took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Mor day amid many manifestations of regret. The cortege assembled at the residence of the deceased, Museum- street, at noon and headed by a posse of police under the command of the Chief Constable proceeded to the Cemetery where a brief service conducted by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton was held in the Chapel. The chief mourners present were Mesap.H. P. C. White,G, F. F. White and E. A. White,and amongst others also in at- tendance were Alderman Pierpoiat,Bennett, Smethurst and John Roberts,Councillors Skelland Dickenson and Dr. Joseph,Mr. J. Moore Murray (director of education) Mr.F. Turner(superintendent of the fire brigade),Mr.W. F. Flood (inspector of nuisances), Mr. F. W. Purse (representing the electricity depat Lrent), Mr. T. H. Spinks and Mr, W. T. Crank (representing the Town Clerk's department), Messra. John Fairclough, S. Wright, H. Thornton,and R. T. Fcirclough (represent- ing the Rorough mazistrates), Mess 13. T. J. Ridgway, W. Parsons, Robert Dobson, W. Owen, A. Browne, E. L. Flemming, S. Barlow, F. J. Haddock, Herbert Woods, NV. Holbrook, P: Brimelow, Dr. Peacock, and the Revs. T. W, Barker and T. R. Wild. The Free- masons were represented by Bros. C.' Lce, W.M., (Lodge of Lights, 148), J. McDowell, W.M., (Gilbert Greenal Lodge 1250) and H. F. Greenough W.M., (Lodge of Friendship 2963). The Mayor was repre- sented by Mr. Fowls. -The service at the graveside was conducted by the Rev. E.C.E. Carleton. A num- ber of beautiful floral tributes were plauxl on the grave including those by:—", His children," Mrs. Peter Rylands and Miss Rylands, Mr. and Mrs. Fleming, Councillor and Mrs. Fulton Smith, Dr. and Mrs. Braide, Dr. and Mrs. Peacocke, Dr. and Mrs. Bowden, Mrs. J. Harrison and Miss Atkinson, Mr. and Mrs. R. Silcock, Mr. and Miss Haddock, and "Sarah." Galvanized Roofing Sheets, Roofing Felts, &amp;c. Large stook.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at, Warrington.—Advt. Cromwell and Warrington. A paper was read on Monday evening at the Liver- pool Literary and Philosophical Society's meeting in the Royal Institution, Colquitt-street. Mr. Roland, J. A. Shelley, F. R. list, S.,on " Cromwell in Lancashire and the reasons that led to his presence there." Mr. Shelley said that the county of Lancashire had played an important part in the first Civil War. Nowhere had the King a more loyal or powerful adherent than James seventh Earl of Derby, but against this no- where outside the capital was Presbysterianism more flourishing or rampant. To be brief, the great Roman Catholic and Anglican families of the two West ern Hundreds of South Lancashire, e.g., the Stanleys, Earls of Derby, Molineux of Sefton, Wardens, Haighs, Dnxburys, Blundells of Crosby and Ince, Norises of Spoke, Scarisbricks of Scarisbrick, were ardent Royalists, though individual members ram have fought for the other side. In the Northern Hundreds, too, the balance of power lent to the King's cause. Wigan was the headquarters of Royalist influence in the county, and the Puritans reckoned it the most malig- nant town in Lancashire. It was a regrettable truth that the warfare between the two parties was waged with a greater acrimony in our own county than, perhaps in any other in England. Mr. Shelley gave interesting details with regard ta the fighting in the neighbourhood of Warrington, and said that, fortun- ately for the memery of Cromwell, Monroe showed no desire to hurry south, though had he done so, as urgently advised by Tyldesley, and given fight to the exhausted Parliament troops at Warrington, it was quite conceivable a different issue might have resulted to the invasion. The cam- paign ended in a triumph for Cromwell and his army. Considering the disparity between the two forces, it was indeed a great military feat, for if they might accept Gromwell's own figures, whereas the Scots total numbers and those of the allied. English Royalists amounted to 24,000 men, his own were 9,000 at the utmost. After an epoch-making visit of less than a fortnight, Cromwell quitted Lancashire, and his statue at Warrington looked down on the scene of his memorable triumph. It might not be altogether vain to hope that the softening influence of time had helped our generation of Lancashire people to forget the imperfections of his character, to which the finest nature was prone, and to recollect with pride how honourably this county was identified with the memory of him who had been aptly termed our Chief of Men." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S WARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Two Candidates in the Field.</p>
        <p>On Tue-.day next the burgesses of St. John's Ward will be called upon to elect a repre-entative on the Town Council to fill the vacancy occasioned by the elevation of Councillor J. Rol 3rts to an aldermanic seat. There are two candidates in the field—Mr. Henry Thornton (Non-Trader) and Mr. James Pdl (Tabour)— and considerable interest is being taken in the election. On Monday Mr. William Furaesa (Liberal) was also nominated but he has since withdravc a in favour of Mr. Thornton, to whom he sent the following lettsr " Fern Le?," Great Sankey, Near Wa(rington. Feb. 13, 1905. H. Thornton, Esq., J.P. Dear Sir,—Having carefully considered my position as an official of the Liberal Association, I have come to the conclusion that it would be very incoJsistent on my part to put myself in opposition to another fellow- Liberal—and one older than myself. I have therefore with the consent of my propo..3r viithdrassu my nomiratiols.</p>
        <p>Yours Faithfully, W. FURNESS.</p>
        <p>NON-TRADERS AND MR. BALL.</p>
        <p>The following correspondence hes taken pla.2e between Mr. James Dodds and Mr. 13111 : " Warrington Non-trading Association. Penketh House, near Warrington, 10th February, 190 G. Mr. James Ball, 127, Marsh House-lane, Warrington. Dear am requested to ask you the followiog questions :- 1. Are you of opinion that no representative elc2ted to any public body should derive pecuniary profit, either directly or indirectly, by reason of his membership of such public body ? •2. Will you,if elected as a member of the Warrington Town Council, vote against the election of a-1y person as a memb2r of the Education Committee, or of any other committee, who is interested directly or indirectly in any contract with such committee, or with the Corporation? Will you k,ndly le;-. me have a reply by first post after receipt hereof ?—Yours faithfully, JAMES DODD,S, Chairman."</p>
        <p>" 127, Marsh Hous: Wa.,ington, February 10th, 19N. Dear sir,—l base your letter of even date, and beg to state tla I dr.:line to answer your questions in the categoric' winner in which they are cast. Neither 'No ' nor 'Yes would fairly represent my views on the matter.</p>
        <p>"Jam in favour of purity of municipal government and administration, but I think the end would be better attained by the extension of the system of direct em- ployment, &amp;c., by the Corporation on municipal works than by the geaeial adoption of the remedy advocated by the Non-trading ssociation.—Yours faitbfully JAMES BALL. Mr. James Dodds, Penketh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>Council and the Unemployed.</p>
        <p>THE PAINTING OF THE MARKETS</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Wet rington Tract( and Labour Council was held in the Bold-street schools on Tue-alay Mr. Plumb presiding over a bir attendance of delegates.</p>
        <p>THE- REPRESENTATION ON THE EDUC ATION COMMITTEE:</p>
        <p>- Councillor STorr read a letter from the Town Clerk pointing out that the term for which Mr. Plinston was appointed a co-opted member of the Education Com- mittee, as reresenting the Trades Council. expired in March. and asking that the Councillor should nomilat3 a successor.</p>
        <p>Mr. GREALEY propc-ed the retiring representative be re-elected. The Trades Council could not possibly select a more suitable rrgu, (Hear, hear). Mr. LUCAS secorded. . _</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT supported the motion. He thought Pliaston should be thanked for his excellent ser- vices on behalf of the Couecil. Mr. Plinston had had a very difficult work to perform and had cariied it out with credit to himself and the Council.</p>
        <p>The motion was unanimously of*. ied. Mr. I ..I TON epr i his thanks for his rc election The position was not always a very plea..ant one. He had often had to pat up with the bugetirgs of members of the commit; •_••re w.th morey -al often more imprudence —(Mr. C c ard le, a brans")—but he I would endeavour to c) y out I',e duLir wit% credit to , himself and with sLec;ion t 3 the Council. During the three yca,J wh'c', Fe had ..ervcd on the r emmittt he hal only ma. i tbrc3 Yet ;tinge including the attendance every Toursday afte.2oo ) at the meetings of the most impo• tent suh-commitice. 'lnes/ had however something to look forward to. It was quite possible that his tenure of °Tee urde.;...he pre. 2 3t reg;rne I would be short br .1`..(3 he believed that one of the ; amendments to the p r -At Ee.7C),L'Orl Act would he the important step of providing 11- •t a men should 13 sent to serve on that C inemitt 3 ash a mandate from the town. (Hear, bee.) He RO l'd i.itbe erve el a representative of er. p tye • thrn 1 : teat from the Trades Council became they we metim • told by the important indiv'cluels 0 1 commit 3 who happered to be Councillors V- ,t they (the Councillors) were elected by the pee .'e, r the othees were 0.,1y co-opted members. he admit,- cd the importlnr 3of toe Councillors, but they Wei e not the o fly important people in the town. (flea-, heir.) He ,metirrc^ told that be had no man&amp; te I Nal the i ep'e, but he helievci that in the future the rape 131: 1 on the committee would be spec-r'ly elected by the ras..7 tyet.3. If that did come the Labour party ner-t see that t'tey had not only one repre,..enaJve on t committee, tut more than one to look after their interests A big step would he taken the following day by the laying of the founda- tion stone of one of the new schools—a step which ought to have been taken some time ago, so as to pro- videaccommodation for the education of the children of the town. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>L.R.C. REPORT.</head>
        <p>L.R.C. REPORT.</p>
        <p>l‘fr. CORMS, delegate to the Labour Representation Committee, presented his report of the procc2dings at the lest meeting of the Committee. The question was raised as to the suggestion of the Trades Council to con- vene a meeting to form a Right to Work Committee, and it was decided to wait for the Trades Council to move in the matter, provided they were not too long. With rewd to the municipal byc-elect,or, the cotnmitt decided to take no action but to work so as to secure bettev ovganizat'on for the next November election. The qrest ion was :.tised as to the peralties to be ',Meted upon socie:.ic- p:oposing candidat-3 who turred out to be ineligible. It was proposed that the Society nominating an irellgible esndidate should pay all the expentes incurred but it WAS felt that this would cause trouble and would probib'y result in societies refraining from nom i eating candidates, and it was decided to draw up a iis of questions to be sub- mitted to candidates before their adoption. At the meeting the whole of the (Akers of the committee were re-elected.</p>
        <p>RIGHT TO WORK COMMITTEE. Connelllor STorr explained that at the last mc3ting of the Council it was decided to convene a conference, to which representatives of the whole of the trade union branches in the town would bo invited, including the Labour Representation Committee and the Indepen- dent Labour Party for the purpose of forming a Right to Work Comm;tiee. He howeve hod not taken soy steps in regal to calling tbe ir• oa account of the general elecLion end ill-health. In Warringto about 120 unemployed had registe:ed t,,eir names and at that time somethin7 like 110 were requiring work. The Distress Committee had held a meeting the previous wo 3it when it was felt that the,e WAS rot sn'ticient in Warring- ton to warrant the commiti Ne in makim; an appeal to the public for flooncill o-sistance. He felt sometimes that the time spent in attending the mee,ings of the com- mittee was wasted because they could not provide work through lack of funds. They cotod not ask for assist- ance from the Quen's fund until they had done some work and they could not do any work until they got some money and, they were in a di licutty as they felt that they could not appeal for sub •ci iptions with so few unemployed ia the toao. It WJ9 bad that they should have so many unen]ployc 1 on their hands A id not be able to do anything and like 1111cawber they were waiting for something to turn up. He did not know whether the present Government would do mything but under the existing act they were so restricted with regulations that they could do nothing. Mr. GREALRY proposed that the meeting be celled and that the Trades Council be represented by two delegates, other societies to be represented in likepro- portionas they wee represented on the Trades Council. After SOMA discussion Mr. Hanley's motion wol carried and Councillors Stott and the Chairman were appointed to represent the Council. It was decided to convene the meeting on or before March COUNCILLOR STOTT_AND THE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>CONDI TWEE. Councillor STOTT referred briefly to the paintinof the markets. The Markets Cominittca, he said, origin- ' ally advertised for tendel3 for the work but it Wes ultimately decided to do the work by direct labour, it being thought that the work would be better and more cheaply done noder this systsm. That was a vary important step and he NN.1.4 anxious that it should La successful so that other committns of the Corporation would be encouraged to act upon simil ir lines. There was no reason, in his opinion, why the Corporation should not do a great deal of work dirc:it, as if they did so work could be provided all the year round for a cer- tain number of joiders, painters and others. H 3 sincerely approved of the step taken by the Markets Committea. Thirty painters were out of work and it was decidcl to employ them all by engaging them alteinate weeks and having ten working at one time. 'be master painters were not looking with a kindly eye upon the departure as the work was being done too well and too quickly.</p>
      </div>
