      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>The now Parliament, THE NEW which came into being PAULI AMEN T. on Thursday, will dis- appoint all expectations if it does not prove to be one of the most notable Parliaments on record. Even the party which was so badly beaten at the polls expects it to do groat things. Men like Mr. BALFOUR and Sir E WARD CLARKE speak as if it were fore-doomed to ruin the Empire. How much of the accent of sincerity there is in such apprehensions we do not pretend to say, but however sincere they might be, they would be none the less ill-founded and unreason- able. A reasonable prophecy would be that this will be an eminently sane and practical Parlia- ment. It will insist on doing many things that have hitherto only been talked about, and doing them with an expedition that is new to Parlia- mentary methods. There was an example on Tuesday of the spirit with which the new House of Commons approaches its labours. Mr. Lowruca was elected to the Speakership by an unanimous vote. Nobody thought it worth while to recall the fact that Mr. GULLY'S nomination to the chair was not only con- tested, but by the whole Conservative party,in the first instance, and that his seat at Carlisle was con- tested with the approval and as sistance of Mr. Berarotus at the next ensuing General Election. IMr.LOWTHER is a pronounced Conservative. He holds a seat which would certainly have been won from him if his Liberal Opponent, who was a great favourite in the constituency,had not retired when Mr. LowntEn was elected to the Speaker- ship. If the Conservatives had scored such a victory as that whick has just fallen to the Liberal party, it cannot be doubted that a change would have been made had a Liberal Speaker chanced to occupy the Chair at the opening of a new Parliament. The career of the. new House of Commons, it is not improbable, will be less determined by itself than 'by the House of Lords. Nobody is so optimistic, we suppose, as to believe that the Peers will act on the excellent advice given to them the other day by the statesman who was known not long ago as Sir MICHAEL HICKS-BEACH. Most of them, it is likely enough, will resent any advice from such a quarter. They will probably say that a politician who has just been raised to the peerage should, for the present, be seen and not heard, especially on matters of this intimate and domestic kind. Yet the advice of the new .peer was not only highly necessary under the conditions created by a great Liberal victory,but the very best that could be given. The House of Lords wantonly destroyed almost the whole of the work done by the 1892-5 Govern- ment. The proceeding was so serious that a collision between the two Houses seemed at one time imminent. Had Mr. GLADSTONE Ibeen a younger man the difficult ques- tion of the relative power of the two I Houses might have been settled once for all. As it happened, the great statesman contented him- self with the issue of an eloquent and solemn warning to the Peers in what proved to be his last speech in the House of Commons. If one thing more than another can be safely predicted of the new House of Commons, it is that it will not permit the experience of 1892 to 1895 to be repeated. It will insist that its measures !shall go through both Houses. What is advised by Sir MICHAEL HICKS BEACH is that the Lords shall recognize the fact that the new House of Commons exists for legislative purposes, and that it is the people, rather than the House of Lords,who must determina what measure shall be passed into law. He urges forbearance tied even compliance—that is to say his policy is a discreet one, and by pursu-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>ing it the House of Lords would probably outlast the new Parliament. It cannot outlast it in its present form and t rtitution on any other terms.</p>
        <p>In presiding over a meeting ALDERMAN BENNETT of the Lancashire and AND THE Cheshire Conservative ELECTIONS. Workingmen's Federation at Manchester on Saturday, Alderman Bennett delivered a speech the like of which W 9 hardly think he would have uttered in , his native town of Warrington. It had appar- ently been Tfrepared for the occasion, and tha statements in it were of the sort which are known to go down easily and acceptably with the Con- servative workingman. For instance the worthy Alderman said—with uncommon virulence for him —that "the Liberals had put forth a number of lying statements and placards." Now we presume that he was speaking from experience—an experi- ence gained in the course/of the election in War- rington—and we should be glad if he would specify a few of the "lying statements and placards '•; which were published by the Liberals. We know there were some unpleasant home truths empha- sized which our political opponents, with that courteous diction for which they are famous stig- matised as "Radical lies," and "More Radicahies, ' and they issued poster rejoinders which, as regards party colours, were lies on the face of them. We do not know of any Liberal "lying statements" which compare in enormity and baseness with the foreign labour canard so industriously circulated by Alderman Bennett's friends, but possibly he wilt enlighten us. In any case it is unlike Alder- man Bennett, as one of the defeated party, to say that the other side won by the agency of "lying statements." He has the reputation of being able to take a beating in better temper. Mr. Bennett himself, in this particular speech, makes some rather rash assertions which other people might describe in stronger terms without his having any right to complain. For instance, he said that instead of the result of the election— and he was now speaking generally—being a victory for Free Trade " it was nothing of the sort." Surely " lying statements on Chinese Labour and other subjects " did not cause the wonderful revulsion of popular feeling which was manifested in every part of the British Isles with the exception of the "Birmingham compound?" These siue issues were not responsible for the overthrow of men like Mr. A. J. Balfour, Mr. Gerald Balfour, Mr. Henry Chaplin, Mr. Boner lksw and other well-known tamperers with tariffs. Alderman Bennett must know this as well as we do, and it is unfortunate for him that the speech intended for the nicely-attuned ears of the Conservative workingmen has been submitted to a larger and more critical public. He refers also to the necessity for educating the masses as to "the folly of throwing themselves out of work in favour of the foreigner'B. free imports." We . . _</p>
        <p>should like to help him in.educating the masses,for the more they are enlightened and the sooner we shall see the conversion of the minority to-day into the belief of the majority that in free imports lies England's salvation and comfort and happiness. It is free imports that ensure the great boon of cheapness, and the fact will be more widely recognised as the masses are educated. The Alderman is "looking backward " when he advances such worn-out shibboleths for the con- sideration of a democracy which has already begun to think for itself. We suggest that he finds more substantial mental food for the consumption of the Conservative Workingmen's Federation if that incongruous body lives long enough to hold another annual meeting.</p>
        <p>With the approach of THE RATES. March anxious eyes are turned in the direction of the Town Hafluid vague and, generally speaking, adverse views are entertained as to the possibility of the long-suffering, ratepayer being relieved of some small portion of his burden. The most opti- mistic dared but hope that the rates would not increase and by these, as by all, the preseiitation of the estimates of the Education Committee will 'be received with gloomy forebodings. It was hardly to be expected that the requirements of this large spending body would decrease, but an increase of nearly 5:3,000 in the estimates for the coming year will occasion something in the nature of a shock, as it means that the education rate will advance by a little over 2d. in the 2. The increase in the deficit to be met out of the Cor poration funds is in a large measure due to the automatic advancing of salaries and with several members of the committee we are tempted to ask : where is this going to end P With no relief to come from this quarter, the production of the Borough budget is awaited with anxiety, and it is ' to be hoped that our city fathers, by exercising the most rigid economy and providing only for work which is absolutely necessary, may be able to avert the threatene i advance of the rates. In their efforts towards this end they will be severely handicapped by the decision of the Local Government Board with reference to the Police Buildings. The action of that authority in reduc- ing by over £l,OOO the sum which they permitted the Corporation to borrow is to be deplored in that it involves tte immediate payment of that amount—equal to a penny rate— out of revenue. Confronted with these difficulties it is practically impossible for a reduction to be effected by the Town Council,and on the other hand it is to be feared that the probability of an advance is unpleasantly real.</p>
        <p>"Progress" haE. always been A PROGRESSIVE the watchword of Messrs. CONCERN. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, • Ltd., and their great And flourishing industry at Bank Quay is an evidence of the signal success of their policy. Of late years the growth of the concern has been remarkable and the extensions which have become necessary from time to time have been hailed with satisfac- tion by thetowespeople. A further notabledevelop- meat is nearing accomplishment, and the splendid new offices which will shortly be opened are an orna- ment to the town and a credit to the firm. Beauty and utility have been equally studied and the imposing structure should give delight to such an idealistic body as the "Beautiful Warrington Society," as well as amply fulfilling the firm's requirements. The celebration dinner on Monday was quite a memorable event in the history of the firm. The Borough Member, on the eve of enter- ing upon his Parliamentary duties, attended, and as chairman spoke in a very happy way of the progress and aims of the firm which has certainly set a fine example in social work. A highly interesting record of the early days was presented by Mr. J. J. Crosfield who gave the assembly some idea of the development of the company from a small and struggling concern to a vast industry employing 2,000 hands. In so care- fully studying the well-being of their employees, Messrs. Crosfield have adopted a policy which has commended itself to all right-thinking people, and we hope the opening of the new offices may be the inauguration of an era of ever-increasing prosperity.</p>
        <p>The overcrowding difficulty THE NEW SCHOOL. has been one of the most important which the War- rington Education Committee has had to lace and there is at last a prospect of the urgent demands for day school accommodation being met. On Thursday the Mayor laid the memorial stone of the " Beamont " School in Orford-avenue—the first of two elementary schools which it has become absolutely necessary for the authority to provide. The Board of Education have threatened to withhold grants if their requirements are not complied with, so that the committee has had no loption but to enter upon a building scheme. ! £16,500 is the estimated cost of the Beamont ' School which is to be constructed on the most appproved lines. It was rather lunfortunate that at Thursday's ceremony Alderman Bennett, as chairman of the Education Committee, took occasion to jocularly twit the authority on its "economic tendencies " which he seemed to think hp.d been exercised to the detri- ment of he ornamental features of the building. Alderman Pierpoint was moved to retaliate by emphasising the urgent need • for economy and stating that he had been informed that the work</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>might have been done for several thousand pounds less than the anticipated figure. On the other hand, the Mayor affirmed that the committee was bound to build in the way directed by the Board of Education and that the expenditure was being kept as low as those requirements would allow. For the sake of the heavily-burdened ratepayer we can only hope that this is the case. Education is an expensive necessity to the community and it is imperative that the Committee should be fully alive and attentive to the call for strict economy.</p>
        <p>Before the Unionist party TOEING meeting assembled the THE MAHE. motive for cal I ing it together had disappeard. It was to settle the question of leadership, among other things; but when Conservative Members and Conservative candidates who sustained defeat at the polls were on the way to Lansdowne House they already knew that Mr. Balfour had been reappointed leader by Hr. Chemberlaiti. The party meeting could merely assent. The Mem- ber for West Birmingham, who is not a Con - servative, as he reminded us the other day, has become the absolute and unquestioned dictator of the Tory party. There was something almost royal in the manner in which he accepted Mr. Balfour's submission. "I . . gladly accept the policy which you indicate," and "any services which I can render will be entirely at your disposal." Really, it is like the Lord Chan- cellor conveying the King's 'approval of Mr. Speaker's re-election. Friendly critics, anxious to make Mr. Balfour's fall appear to be "only a little one," point out that there is still muuli ambiguous phrasing in Mr. balfour's expression of his views. It is surely a most equivocal compliment to pay to a public man. One who was capable of consciously arranging his phrases with the view of deceiving those by whom they were to be read would forfeit the last ragged shreds of honour. But there is actually no ground for these interpretations and glosses. Mr. Balfour has at length spoken out with as much clearness and definiteness as is possible to a man with ' his involved type of mind; and he has spoken out in such a way as satisfies Mr. Chacpberlain. Between them, and in agreement with each other, these two politicians have committed the whole Conservative party to a policy of Protection. One of them calls it Tariff Reform and the other calls it Fiscal Reform. It is neither one nor the other, if there Were any real difference ; it is Protection pure and simple. Tariffs, says Mr. Balfour, must not be imposed for the purpose of raising prices. Of course not I Who would be so inexpert as thus to proclaim their actual object? But they would raise prices all the same. In the public estimation of his char- acter Mr. Balfour will pay as heavy a price as has ever ben paid for the leadership of a party. His has not been a gradual development of opiniou, or even a sudden conversion. He has simply been driven by another mind into the acceptance of views which he knows to be dangerous. The taxa- tion of hiod, he said at Sheffield, " is not within the limits of practical politics." Retaliation by means of a general tariff, ho observed on the same occasion " would involve too great a dis- turbance of our practice, and might risk the disorganisation of our trade." Now, with some- thing of a wry face, it may be, he takes the risk. It is fortunate that he cannot at present take any further responsibility. He goes over to Protec- tion when, fortunately, there is nothing for him to lead but a broken party. His submission to Mr. Chamberlain can do no present national harm, and it is for the electors to take care that it does no great harm in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MOTORIST'S DARING EXPLOIT. . Mr. S. F. Bennett Ascends to Arthur's Seat. London "Evening '</head>
        <p>MOTORIST'S DARING EXPLOIT. . Mr. S. F. Bennett Ascends to Arthur's Seat. London "Evening '</p>
        <p>g Standard" of Saturday last account of a motoring exploit at S. F. Bennett, brother of Dr.</p>
        <p>"Everybody in Edinburgh was talking of the ascent of Arthur's seat made by Mr. Charles . Jarrett in a 22 h.-p. Crossley car, on which I had accompanied him the day before. "What do you say to a short run in a little Cadillac ?" said Mr. F. S. Bennett.</p>
        <p>I agreed, as I had up to that time been riding almost exclusively in more or less sumptuous high-powered cars. The little 9 h.-p. Cadillac would be a relief. After passing Holyrood Plalace we came upon the tracks of the Crossley car in the green turf between the road and the foot of the hill path. I pointed out the tracks to Bennett as proof of the feat which all motor- ing Edinburgh was then discussing. Without a word he turned around and headed back for town. Near John Knox's House he stopped at a photo- grapher's, jumped down, and presently he re-appeared accompanied by a man carrying a tripod camera. Then he turned the car towards the hill again, and before I knew it we had left the road and were floundering through the soft, marshy grass just as Jarrott had done the day before, At the very ft of this shelf road—for it is little else than a shelf jutting out from the face of the crag —there is a steep rise where the rock ends and the mushy grass begins. It took two or three attempts to surmount this first obstacle, but surmount it we did,and soon found ourselves headed skyward at an angle of fifteen to twenty degrees. Mr. Bennett was game, and the little Cadillac was still gamer. I felt like an old mountain climber, in view of my experiences of the day before, and I just sat back waiting for the moment when driver and car would be obliged to confess" Thus far and me farther can we go." It was a pretty struggle. The road was never intended for motoring. Its surface is hard rock covered with grass-grown rubble, with occasional boulders blocking the way. Here and there mountain torrents have worn jagged edges in the road, leaving barely sufficient space for the car to pass without the off wheels stumbling into a two-foot-deep gap. One of these we actually dipped Einto with: a front wheel, and the shock as we overlooked the precipitous cliff three or four hundred feet 'deep was one I would not care to repeat. Chug—chug —chug—the little single cylinder puffed and panted on its skyward course. Bennett lay over the steering wheel as though he would coax the little engine to still greater effort. As the skyline, where the road takes an abrupt turn round the cliff, loomed into view I the grey outlines of Holyrood Palace far beneath us, while the spreading, landscape told us we were getting up in the world. It was a hard struggle and a keen one, but we won, and soon had the satisfaction of finding ourselves on the level plateau where we had had our pictures taken the day before. But we had forgotten the photographer. Down at the foot of the hill we could see him with his camera painfully crawling up the steep ascent. "I'll go and fetch him," said Mr. Bennett, and with- out more ado he turned the car downhill. I got off to lighten the load, and soon had the satisfaction of seeing the car back again with the photographer in my place and two extra passengers whom Bennett had picked up seated in the tonneau. There may be some truth in the old adage about " Facilis descensus Averni," but there is " facilis des- census "about Salisbury Crags. The road uphill was bad enough, but it was a boulevard as compared to the sheep path leading downhill. " Did Jarrott go downhill ?" Bennett asked. I laughed at the question. Nothing but a sheep or cat could descend that narrow path, and it must be pretty sure-footed at that. "All right, then, I'll try it," he said, and before I had time to utter a protest we were moving on the down grade. It was too late to object—the only objection which could have availed would have been to get out and walk. But there was barely room even to step off the car without toppling headlong down hundreds of feet. How we did hug the crag side of that road ! Hair-raising is a mild term to express our feelings. We simply sat there numb and helpless, playing the brakes and the reverse for all they were worth against the tremendous odds of that awful descent. This is what I call fine," one of the tramp passengers in our tonneau exclaimed. "Grand! " echoed the other. "These motor-cars are a great invention." They were actually enjoying it! The thought of fools rushing in where angels fear to tread brought the comforting remembrance of another old age about" Fools for luck." We certainly needed all the luck that Providence had to spare, for right ahead of us the road took a most horrible bend, and to make matters worse, sloped at a most uncomfortable angle towards the cliff side. ' Just then, too, the brake which had been beating a tattoo on the drum slipped for an instant. We shot ahead about a foot, and for a fraction of a second it seemed as though we would make a short cut back to Edinburgh—or at least to the foot of the cliff. But quicker than it takes to write it we had slid around that wretched curve, leaning our full weight inwards like circus riders, to offset the slope in the road, and we found ourselves with a road steep, but straight as a toboggan slide, between us and earth. Then we let go, and I doubt if the fastest time made at Ormond Beach could eclipse our record for that last lap • towards terra firma.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. M. kstbury, M.P.,. has presented his own gold watch to one of his chief workerslin coniemora- lion of the Liberal victory in the division at the recent election. * Fearnhead, which is the upper portion of Padgate district, has the reputation of being the healthiest suburb of Warrington. This is according to the medical authorities. .* * *</p>
        <p>There were no coins in the sealed bottle which was deposited in the memorial stone of the Beamont School on Wednesday. Alderman Bennett suggested that this might be another phase of" the fit of universal economy" which is being experienced. In every new House of Commons there are a certain number of won't.- members who sit on their hats after . they have delivered their maiden speech. It is one of those old and familiar jokes that never cease to interest and amuse the House of Cominons.—"M..l.P."</p>
        <p>* * * Therßand of Hope festival at Parr Hall last night( was. a splendid success. We hope it may be taken as an indication of increased zeal in the temperance cause, for there was a crowded attendance and every- one seemed delighted with the pretty spectacle pre- sented.</p>
        <p>* * His Worship the Mayor —who is the most generous of hosts—again poked fun at the Council in the course of the dinner on Wednesday evening We understand, however, that as this was a festive gathering pure and simple his Worship does not desire to be taken seriously, so we have refrained from reporting his remarks verbatim in cold type. * * In another Column will be found the interesting account of a daring motor ride by a local man, Mr. S. F. Bennett, brother of Dr. Bennett, Padgate, who has lately emulated the deed of the famous Jarrott by driving a 9 h.-p. steam car up and down the precipit- ous hill known as Arthur's Seat, Edinburgh. Con- siderable nerve and pluck must have been required for the undertaking, * * The "Dawn" in deploring the utter rout of the Tory party at the general election says sadly : "We had hoped against hope, worked our best, and clung with pathetic faith to the inherent patriotism of the English race." 'What has patriotism to do wit'i the matter? We never heard before that it was a monopoly of the Tory party, but the " Dawn" seems to think so.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. E. Pickavance, the genial secretary of the Cycling Club, was again quite poetic in his annual report presented to the club on Thursday evening. He described the doings of the season in the vivid and picturesque style with which his reports have familiarised the wheelers, and made all long for the summer with its delightful outings in " dear old Cheshire."</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. A. H. Crosileld, M.P., had an enthusiastic send- off on leaving Warrington on Monday to take up his Parliamentary duties. He was present both at the dinner to celebrate the opening of Messrs. Croslield's now offices, and at the congregational entertainment at the Workingmen's Missim. He was warmly compli- mented on his great victory, and as be left each gathering the general well-wishing found expression hearty cheers.</p>
        <p>*5 * t e Mr. E. Plinston, who has posed as a man of many °blurs has only one description as a member of the Ed ' nca.tion Committee. There he is a labour repre- sentative pure and simple, and on Tuesday he was deservedly thanked by the Trades Council for his 1 l'ervices on the education authority. We notice with Interest that he expects the Education Act to be so amended as to give direct popular representation. If it turns out to be correct we shall-have to class him next as a political prophet. . * * * A short time ago some of the Padgate residents thought it would be a wise plan to call the whole dis- trict by one name, and suggested that it should be called Padgate. The Fearnhead residents declined to see the wisdom of this, and pointed but that if there was any change in name it should he called Fearn- head. The latter had more logic in their arguments, for it appears from the records that Fearnhead was long in existence before Padgate, and in fact the latter is really part of the former, the name Padgate having arisen out of a mere "localism." The bile-election lojohn's Ward, caused by the ei is arousing of Councillor John Roberts as an alderman, using Only a minimum amount of interest in the town. Neither Liberals nor Conservatives are offici- ally running a candidate, but there will be a contest ibetween a Labour candidate in the person of Mr. James Ball, and lir . Hem.. Thornton, an ex-member of 1-- the Town Council, who is supported by the Non- ., Traders. Mr. Ball has had some slight experience of , : : municipal work as an elective auditor, and there is every indication of a close fight for the representation ' of the Ward. * * * The Heathens resent the imputation that tree plant- ' . ing in the district is the work of faddists. The mem- , hers of the Parochial Committee at their meeting on : Thursday rounded upon the inhabitant of villadom - who thus stigmatised tire planters, and characterized as ridiculous the suggestion that trees should only be I Placed on footpaths eight feet wide. Nevertheless I they bowed to the superior wisdom of the Rural 1 District Council, and the Surveyor to that Authority in future will have the final voice in the selection of sites and is also empowered to" regulate " any trees which might be considered dangerous. Wednesday was Valentine's Day, but we do not kippose many of our readers were recipients of the ,t'ae-time customary sentimental or humorous valentine It is only a very few years since the shop windows at this time of the year were noticeable by reason of the large stocks of valentines, but it is the exception now to see anything which would remind us of the occa- sion. The custom is all but dead. Even the vulgar and cruel burlesque valentines that outstayed the isentimental ones seem to have made their welcome departure at last. The postal valentine of thirty years ago was itself a mere relic of the old St. Valentine's Day ceremonies. Formerly it was the custom for lads and lasses to draw lots on the eve of St. Valentines , Day to decide who should be their " valentine " for the coming year, and it was expected that the youth .. at least should make a suitable present—the prototype of the postal valentine—to the maiden. * * * Many Warringtonians will be pleased to learn Rod 1 ~ tidings of Mr. J. W. Howarth, late assistant librarian attWarrington. Six months ago Mr. Howarth secured ''. the post of librarian and curator of the Chelmsford " Public Library and Museum. The building was thenl : only in course of . construction, Mr. Andrew Carnegie , having contributed largely towards placing the under- taking on a sound financial basis\ On Saturday, ' •i:, Pebruary 3rd, the building, which is built of red brick , and white stone,was formally opened by Lady Rayleigh ~! and in its finished state it is a credit not only to the - 'benders and architect, but to Mr. Howarth, whip has i Planned pkrsonallv all the internal arrangements. The . 4, Essex County Chronicle" in reporting the opening ~ ceremony and describing the building says ' with i respect to both library and museum, admirable dis- crimination has been shown even to the smallest `, . details by the painstaking Librarian and Curator, , kr. J. W. Howarth." * 5 * . The following style of paragraphing seems .to be titir , ite generally followed by American reporters. It rtainly affords an opportunity for exercising their .; ,Ilgenuity : " A young man in Louisville examined a keg of damaged gunpower with a red-hot poker, to see if it was good. It is believed by his friends that he has gone to Europe, although a man has found some human bones and a niece of shirt-tail about twenty miles from Louisville." "John Smith, inn.. in Nebraska, said be could handle a rattlesnake the same as a snake-charmer. The churlishness of the undertaker in demanding pay in advance delayed the funeral four days." " A circus rider in Arizona tried to turn three somersaults on horseback the other day. The manager sent back to New Orleans for another , somersault man." "A man warned his wife in Chicago not to light the fire with kerosene. She did not heed the warn- ing. Her clothes fit his second wife remarkably • well." "A bey in Canada disregarded his mother's ininnctions not to skate on the river as the ice was thin. His mother does not cook for as many as she formerly did." • "In Mnssachesetts' the other day a man thought . . he could Cross the track in advance of a locomotive. it1)11 The services at the grave were very impressive." "A man in Maryland the other day ate fifteen raw oysters for a wager. The silver trimmings on his coffin cost 12 dols. 35c."</p>
        <p>I;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
