      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events • The Rev. C. H. Hocken, formerly of Warrington, will be the preacher at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday. In the afternoon he will address a meeting, which will be under the presidency of Alderman Tinnion. At a meeting of the Junior Liberal Association on Monday evening, papers on current reviews will be presented by several members. On Friday evening the members will welcome Mr. A. H. Crostield, M.P„ ,at the Liberal Club. The artistes at the popular penny concert to-night (Saturday) will be Miss Carrie Hastings, Miss May Isherwood, Mr. w. Brereton, the Misses E. and L. Pooley and Mr. A. Grounds. The annual congregational gathering in connection with Holy Trinity Church, particulars of which will be found on page 1, will be held on Tuesday evening. The annual meeting of the members of the Warring- ton Permanent Benefit Building Society will be herd in the Cairo-street schools on Tuesday evening week. An exhibition and sale of work done by the inmates of the Warrington Union will be held at Grappenhall Hall on Thursday. The sale will be opened by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. • The third subscription concert for the season will be given by the members of the Warrington Musical Society on Tuesday evening week. An excellent miscellaneous programme will be submitted. The Rev. Robert Black of Wigan, will preach at the Church anniversary services at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday.</p>
        <p>Next week the boards of the Parr Hall will be occu- pied by this society in the annual performances given in aid of the District Nursing Association. The pie:e de resistance will be the favourite play " All that graters is not gold " which by the. way is rather intimately connected with the cotton inaustry. The finishing touches are being given to the production at the final rehearsals and we anticipate that a most suc- cessful performance will be the result. The introduc- tion of the farcical curtain raiser is sure to please and the pieces are likely to run as merrily as the wedding bells. A capital play, talented players and an excel- lent object—the result we trust will be another splendid success.</p>
        <p>The second of the two balls promoted by the War- rington Women's Liberal Association, will be held on Wednesday next in the Lion Hotel, Warrington, and gives prodffse of being a very enjoyable function. The Lion Hotel has no rival locally in respect of its suitability for dancing. Ample accommodation is also being provided for whist, so that the non-dancers may be catered for. Messrs. Pearson and Payton 's orchestra has been engaged to play for dancing from 8 p.m. to 2 a.m. We hope that those Liberals who have not yet made up their minds will apply early for tickets. The prices are ss. (single) and Ss. (double) ; information as to where they may be obtained will be found on our front page.</p>
        <p>We call our readers' attention to the concert to be given by the Warrington Male Voice Choir, who have been fortunate in securing the assistance of the famous Manchester Orpheus Glee Society in addition to several popular artistes, notable among these being Mr. Joseph Hanson, who was greatly appreciated on his last visit to Warrington some considerable time ago.Thelighter element will be well sustained by Mr.O. Campania, a well-known humorist and society enter- tainer, frequenting the London, Ramsgate and district concerts, and who will make his first appearance in Warrington; also Miss Lily Whiteside, contralto Master Tom Barton, R.A.M., violinist, and Mr. E. B North, pianist of Manchester. Of the Manchester Orpheus it need hardly in said they head the list of' male choral societies in the North of England. as was evinced by the enconiums showered upon them at the Blackpool Festival in October last,when the judges,ll r. MeNaught, Mr. Percy Pitt and Mr. S. Coleridge Taylor, awarded them 314 out of a possible 320 marks for the singing of the four test pieces. Our local choir also by their recent victory at the Warrington Eisteddfod on January Ist by 58 out of a maximum 60 marks slimy that they are profiting by the training of the talented conductor of the Manchester Orpheus, viz., Mr. U. S. Nesbitt.With the combined forces of two such choirs a musical treat may be confidently expected. The concert is being run at popular prices, the plan of reserved seats being at Messrs. Miller and Co.'s, jewellers' Sankey-street, where unreserved tickets may also be obtained:</p>
        <p>ASK for CIINNEIGIIA.M'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Index to Inside Pages. PnoE. 2. " A Cruel Temptation," serial. Junior Liberal Meeting. Stockton Heath Builder Prosecuted. 3. Women's Chat and Fashions. Workingmen's Mission. District News. G. Messrs. Crosfield's New Offices : Opening Proceedings. Local Football. "John Chimaman at Home," by Emily C. Carting. • ' 7. Our Local Churches.</p>
        <p>Children's Corner. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET,—Advt. Elective Auditors. The elective auditors for the borough will be elected on March Ist, and Wednesday next is the last date for the receipt of nominations. The retiring auditors are Mr. E. Bradshaw :and Mr. Laurence Bolton. Nomina- tion papers have already been handed in on behalf of Mr. 13olton. ASK for CCINNDIGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—AdVL, Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all comrnunica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington J, R. WOOD, Piano Dealer and Tuner,Friars Gate, and 47, Old ondon-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt. Social. A highly successful social promoted by the mem- bers of the St. Barnabas' Young Men's Bible Class took place in the schools on Friday evening. The room had been wiry tastefully decorated, a motto " Success to our Bible classes "being prominent. The attendance numbered over 100 and the function proved a most enjoyable one. Dancing was indulged in until 1 a.m. and games were also provided. Petroleum Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge st., Warring-</p>
        <p>ton.—Advt. Warrington Blacklister's Troubles. John Wilfrid Delooze, Warrington's only blacklister was charged on Wednesday with drunkenness. He was found by an officer the previous night in Foundry street, lying across the footpath helplessly drunk. He refused to go borne, and was locked up.—The Chief Constable said it was one of the most aggravated cases of habitual drunkenness which came before the Bench. All efforts had been made to reform prisoner, but without avail.—The magistrates bound him over in two sureties of ;£5 each and himself in otobe of ! good behaviour for three months, or in default be was committed to gaol for three months. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Oddfellows' Dinner. The annual dinner in connection with the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfellows was held at the Pelican Hotel on Thursday evening, a large number sitting down to an excellent repast served by Mr. Chalmers. Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, Prov. GM., presided over the subsequent proceedings, which were of an enjoyale character. A number of toasts were honoured and songs &amp;c., were given by Bros. T. Reddish, PG., A. Boyle, P.P.G.M., J. Massey, P.P.G.M., J. Willett, Tyler, and P. P. Forbes. During the evening PG., G. F. Freeman was pres4nted with a gold medal in recognition of his work on behalf of the social side of the Lodge activities, and Bro. W. Holbrook, Prov. 'D.G.M., was the recipient of .a, certi- ficate of merit.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>COST OF EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>THE COST OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATES FOR THE COMING YEAR.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee's estimates for the sear 1906-7 have just been drawn up and will be submitted for approval at the monthly meeting on Monday even- ing. The following is a summary of the figures : Committee. Expenditure. Receipts. Deficiency. £ a. d. s. d. £ s. d. School Attend. 1,226 15 5 14 4 0 1,212 11 5 School Manage. 34,597 9 4 29,961 14 0 4,635 35 4 Sites 8: Build'gs 335 0 0 335 0 0 Finance</p>
        <p>44.858 17 4 30,125 18 0 14,732 19 4 There is thus a total estimated deficiency of £14,732 19s. 4d. as against £11,843 75. 4d. for the year just closed. The School Mniagement Committee estimates its expenditure on higher education at £5007 13s. as against £4884 10s. id, estimated for last year. The sum includes £3,542 13s. for teachers salaries and £4OO for the possible grant to the Grammar School. The deficiency in this depart- ment is estimated at £l3OO 13s. as against £1292 Is. On elementary education the expenditure is put down at £29.589 16s. 4d. es compared with £27713 7s. Gd. for 1905. Day school teachers' salaries will absorb £27,222 19s. Bd. and the salaries paij in respect of the cookery classes will be £250 16s. Bd. The estimated deficiency is £3.33525. 4d. as compared with £1,720 6s. 3d. for 1905. The Finance Committee allows a sum of £991 lls. 101. for interest and instalments on Technical Institute loans and £1389 for interest and instalments for loans on the new Bolton and Beamont Schools (land and buildings.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>NEW RATE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW RATE.</p>
        <p>A rate of a penny in the 1£ in Warrington produces £965 and as the deficiency to be met is £E1,732 9s. 4d. a rate of is. 3d. in the £ will be required. Last year's rate was Is. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>SoctAL.—On Saturday evening in response to a kind invitation of Mr. Councillor Brough upwards of 80 members and friends connected with the Wesleyan Bible and evening classes assembled in the schoolroom. After tea, a very pleasant evening was spent. The first portion comprised a concert when the following artistes contributed items with considerable success—all receiv- ing encores. Miss Gandy, contralto, Mr. L. Wood, violinist, Mr. Pearse, base, Mr. Arthur Griffiths, tenor. Mr. Furness, reciter, and Mr. Mellor jun., pianist. As there is a Book Society connected NC it ti the class,during the interval the members were handed the books which they had subscribed for during the ,year. During the evening Mr. Plinston moved a resolution thanking Mr. Brough for his hospitality and remarked that he and his fellow members did not simply look upon Mr. Brough as their leader, but more as a friend, and trusted that he would he long spared to continue his labours in their midst. This was seconded by Mr. Cutland who said he could thoroughly endorse all that had been slid by Mr. Plinston. The resolution was put to the meeting and heartily carried. In responding, Mr. Brough stated what a pleasure it was for him to meet them and that it was also a delight for him to see people enjoying themselves in a truly rational manner. Referring to the class he stated that he was proud of it and could bear testimony to the able papers and addresses which had been given during the year. He felt that he ought to mention the help he had received from Messrs. Plinston, Hall, Seed, England and others and with regard to the Hon. Secretary, Mr. Hague, the latter appeared to find no work too hard to do and did it really well. The membership now numbered between 30 and 40 and he would be pleased to have the number augmented. Mr. Mellor, the resident minister, officiated as chairman, and by his happy and homely remarks contributed to the success of the gathering. The remainder of the evening was spent in games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>4. ‘ MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S NEW , OFFICES.</head>
        <p>4. ‘ MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S NEW , OFFICES.</p>
        <p>Description of the Building. • The new offices of Messrs. Joseph Crosfleld and Sons, tetniotunraen. dThweilblufioldrminga a ma reeaddi rapidlytio rt nearing is a commodious one and will provide ample accommo- dation for the clerical staff of the Bank Quay Works. 1 loca lc ao rirci hp The architectural style adopted is the Georgian phase of the Italian Renaissance and the new premises are faced externally with thin Ruabon bricks relieved with Shrewsbury stone dressings, cornices, copings S:c. 11A special feature of the elevation facing Liverpool- road is the well executed carved stonework over the entrance doorway and on the gables on each side. The 'entrance vestibule, corridor, staircase hall, and general 'office, whilst being simply and conveniently arranged have been elaborately decorated with polished mahogany panelling, and enriched by fibrous plasterwork. The staircase hall at the first floor level and the general office adjoin but are divided by a glazed mahogany screen and together form an apartment 143 feet in length. The staircase hall consists of a central apace 24 feet square containing the main staircase and is sur- mounted by a dome, tilled with stained glass incorpora- ting painted draped male figures symbolizing chemistry, industry, commerce, and science. Surrounding the central space is an arcaded cortidor with an elabor- ately vaulted ceiling. Access is gained from this corridor to the directors' rooms, board room,waiting, interview- ing, and typists' rooms and general office which are arranged round the staircase hall. The general office which will be furnished throughout in polished mahogany is a lofty room with a segmental pannelled and glazed ceiling. The walls are pannelled to a height of 11 feet with polished mahogany and fibrous plasterwork. Above the pannelling is a deep freize which will be decorated with the arms, in gesso work, of 25 of the countries to which the firm sends its products. Above the freize is a heavy dentilled and enriched cornice from which its ceiling springs. Arranged along each side of the office are private rooms for the use of heads of departments. The workmen's dining hall on the ground floor level is a large well-lit room which well also be used for meetings, concerts, lectures, and has a seating capacity of 2,000. Attached to this room is a large kitchen fitted with up-to-date gas cooking apparatus capable of dealing with cooking for many hundreds of employees. The consideration of the firm for the welfare of their workpeople is further indicated by the provision of a large lavatory and bath-room adjoining one of the entrances to the dining hall. The baths and lavatories hem, as well as elsewhere in the building, are supplied with hot and cold water. The heating, lighting and ventilation arrangements of the building are of the most complete character. The heating is effected by hot water, the lighting by electricity and the ventilation by means of electrically driven fans. An extensive system of telephones and electric bells has been installed by the firm's electrical ' engineer and his staff who have also carried out the 'electric lighting arrangements in an efficient and artistic manner. The general work is being carried out by Mr. C. W. Davenport to the designs of Mr. A. Warburton.</p>
        <p>A report of the dinner to celebrate the kormal open: log of the °likes will be found on page 6.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S "MAYFAIR" BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR AFTERNOON TEA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 17,1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 17,1906.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Shop Licence Refused.</p>
        <p>The annual Licensing Sessions for the Daresbury Petty Sessional Division were held at Daresbury Court House on Tuesday when the magistrates present were— Messrs. W. Long (in the chair), J. White. H. Neild,J.M. Edelsteu and Colonel Ridgway.</p>
        <p>POLICE REPORT. Superintendent Ennion reported that there were in the division 23 licensed victuallers, one beprseller, and two off-licence holders,giving an average of 363 persons to each licence. One licensed victualler had been pro- ceeded against during the year the case being dis- missed. Eleven persons had been charged with drunken- ness all being convicted, as compared with 15 proceeded against and 12 convicted the previous year. Seven of the persons convicted for drunkenness were residents in the district the remainder being strangers. Mr. Long said the magistrates were very pleased with the report from which it would appear that the public houses in the district were being used for their legiti- mate purpose. They felt that the licences were doing a good work so long as they were conducted respect- ably and for the benefit of the people. Public-houses should be houses of use and resort so long as they kept within legitimate bounds.</p>
        <p>SHOP-LICENCE REFUSED. Mr. Browne applied for a wine and spirit licence on behalf of Mr. H. W. Turton, chemist, Victoria-square, Stockton Heath. He pointed out that without such a licence his client could not sell medicated wines which were used so extensively, and it was, principally, to enable him to do this that the application was made. No doubt it would be said that if there was plenty of accommodation in the village for the supply of wines and spirits. The only places where these could be obtained were Mulberry Tree Hotel and the Red Lion Inn, both were most respectably conducted houses but some people did not like going into public houses, and there was not within reasonable distance a shop where wine and spirits could be obtained. Mr. Tui.,on said he desired to be placed on a fair footing with other chemists in being able to supply medicated wines</p>
        <p>Mr, W. H. .J. Jenkins said he had been instructed to oppose the application on behalf of Mr. Joseph Shilcock licensee of the Mulberry Tree Hotel, Mr. William Coppock, licensee of the Red Lion Inn, and Mr. A. Cook, licensee of the Bridge Inn. Those three licenses were in close proximity to the centre of the , village and nothing could be urged on the score of their non-accessibility. The three gentlemen whom he repre- sented sll contributed largely to local and imperial taxes, and that was one reason why the magistrates should not grant facilities for opposition. There were members of the community who made excuses and desired facilities for purchasing wine and spirits at a grocer's or chemist's shop. This was to the detriment of themselves and their families, as it con- tributed to secret drinking. A man should be manly enough if he wanted a drink to go in a respoctable public house for it.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Ennio]] opposed the application on behalf of the police. Mr. Long said the magistrates were of opinion that sufficient facilities existed, and refused the application</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Annual Congregational Gathering.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea and concert lit connec- tion with the Warrington Workingmen's Mission was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening. About 180 sat down to a substantial repast, prepared by the lady members of the congregation. Tea was followed by a concert the proceedings being presided over by Councillor James Evans, who was supported on the platform by Mrs. Crosfield, Mr. N. Hough, and Mr. T Dale. An excellent progarmme was submitted. Mr. A. TILLING, in presenting the annual report, said: "It can be safely said that the Mission has never been in a more flourishing state than it is at the present time. With regard to finances, we have again to report a liberal response to the calla that have been made upon the members of the congregation during the year. In the annual report last year it was stated that only £5O had been raised towards the cost of the new organ—£2os—and the hope was expressed that the balance would be raised by the end of this year. This hope has been fulfilled, as the debt was cleared off early in November. The chapel accounts show a balance in hand of .£3 19s. 10d., as compared with ,C 2 2s 2d. at the end of 1904. In addition they had the sum of 9 16s. 3d. in hand towards the decoration of the organ. In con- clusion, Mr. Tilling said : "We are expecting that very shortly the Chapel will appear much more beautiful. For years we have had to chronicle, in our report, the kindness and generosity of our friends bearing the honoured name of Crosfield. During the past year we have again had their encouraging support and sympathy, and in the future iwe are assured of a continuance of their interest in the work of the Mission, as Mrs. Crosfield has intimated her desire to place a beautiful stained glass window in the Chapel in memory of our ' ,te patron, Mr. Crosfieli It does</p>
