      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CA UGHT IN THE ACT. Patrick Connolly, 15, of 23, Water-street, was at the St. Helens Police Court on Thursday, bound over under the First Offenders' Act for stealing 211bs. of brass, valued at us,, the property of Councillor J. France. He stole the brass from one of the outbuildings at l‘fr. France's mill and secreted it in the entry on Tuesday night. Yesterday morning he was in the act of taking it away when he was arrested by Police-constable Watson, who had been keeping watch. FATAL ST. HELENS COLLIERY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>- - - Mr. Brighhouse, county coroner, held an inquest on Saturday afternoon, at the Clock Face Inn, on the body of Thomas Whalley, aged fifty-eight. of 222, Robins-lane, 'Sutton, who was killed by a fall of roof at the Bold IColliery on Thursday. William Harrison, deceased's drawer, said he was working with deceased chipping the roof off in order to put some timber under it, when the roof fell on deceased and a man named French, without the slightest warning, and deceased was killed. French was injured and unable to attend the inquiry. At this particular place the roof was bad owing to the fact that they were nearing an old working place. Samuel Evans fireman, said he visited deceased about noon, and the accident happened at half-past two. When he visited the place he gave instructions to deceased to timber immediately he had finished chipping, and by what witness saw after the accident deceased did not appear to have done any timbering at all. —A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned. ST. HELENS PRIZE HOMERS. The annual show of homing pigeons, held by the St. Helens and District Federation, took place on Saturday at the Fleece Hotel, St. Helens. There were 240 birds shown, and the judge was Mr. R. Slack. The special prize for the best cock bird was won by W. Earnshaw, and the best hen by W. Wainwright. Awards : Cock, flown over 250 miles : 1, E. Burrows ;2, J. A. Rhodes ; 3, J. R. Hughes. Hen: I, C. Dumbell ; 2. B. Brereton ; 3, J. Ittioes ; cock, flown over 150 miles : I, W. Earn- shaw ; 2, W. E. Scarisbrick ' • 3, E. Burrows. Hen : 1, Dumbell and Johnson: 2, J.Penny ; 3, J. R. Hughes. Cock, likeliest flyer: 1, J. Kitts ; 2, J. Fenney ; 3, N. Mercer. Hen : 1, E. Burrows ; 2, J. Kitts ; 3, H. Jackson. Cock, 1905, flown over 80 miles : 1, W. Barn- thaw; 2, M. Cooke ; 3, Coad Brothers. Hen: 1, J. A. Rhodes ; 2, J. Parkinson ; 3, Coad Brothers. Likeliest Cock, any age: 1, Silcock and Crompton ; 2, R. , Briggs; 3, J. Ashworth. Hen : 1, W. Wainwright; 2, J. B. Hughes ; 3,Whittle and Carter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEW CONSERVATIVE CLUB. The Conservative Workingmen of Haydock have just established a Conservative Club in their district--quite a new departure for a colliery village. A meeting has already been held, at which a provisional committee was formed, and it was unanimously decided to form a Workingmen's Conservative Club on the same lines as in other districts, and the enrolment of members has already commenced. The committee unanimously elected Mr. C, R. Pilkington as their first president, Mr. John Robinson chairman of the committee, and Mr. John Jackson vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>LABOUR CELEBRATIONS AT EARLESTOWN. On Saturday evening the victory of Mr. J. A. Seddon, Labour m.P. for the Newton Division, was celebrated by a monster tea party, concert and dance, the latter taking place at the Earlestown Town Hall.—Mr. W. Speakman presided.—Krs. Seddon was presented with a beautiful bduquet of flowers symbolising the Labour colours.—Mr. Seddon, who had a great reception, said theirs was one of the greatest victories in the British Isles. They had had to fight tremendous forces—the power of money, the tyranny of capital in its worst form of intimidation. Their opponents had a perfect organisation, and for a long series of years had experi- enced uninterrupted victory, whereas the Labour party started without organisation, and no money, and in a scattered area it looked almost a miracle that they had achieved victory. He advocated the Labour party taking an active interest in the coming municipal elections, and to strive to attain a perfect organisation. —The social part of the proceedings was a great SUCCeSs. EARLESTOWN MYSTERY SOLVED. The Earlestown police, under Inspector Innes, on Wednesday unravelled a mystery which has occupied their attention for twelve days. On Friday night, the 2nd inst., Mary Elizabeth Williams (38), Nrife of a collier named Barnsley Fisher Williams, of 200, Common- road, Earlestown, disappeared, taking with her their five-year-old adopted daughter, Elizabeth Caroline Williams, a niece. Mr. and Mrs. Williams, it is said, had had a dispute. The husband went to bed, but about nine o'clock that night the woman, with the child, locked up the house, and leaving the key with a neigh- bour, remarked that if she was not at home in the morning her body would be found at the bottom of the canal. Mrs. Williams' disappearance and the receipt of a letter led the sister to communicate with the police, who dragged the canal, and on the following day they recovered the woman's body. At the inquest the jury returned the verdict of suicide.—On Wednesday morn- ing as a man, named Gregson, was walking along the towing path of the canal he saw a child's bonnet in the water. Inspector Times and P. C. Lennie dragged the water in the neighbourhood, with the result that the child's body was recovered shortly before three o'clock.</p>
        <p>SEAGULLS AT SALE. A flock of seagulls has taken up quarters on the flooded meadows at Sale, thirty miles inland, having probably come along the Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>t \ •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CROWNING THE • TEMPERANCE QUEEN. BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CROWNING THE • TEMPERANCE QUEEN. BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM</p>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, 1113. ---- His First Day in Parliament.</p>
