        <p>For careworn, hard-pressed mothers of children what a boon! What a real boon not to need to drudge on in the old way of washing clothes and cleaning house.</p>
        <p>Through the long, long list of matters of household cleaning and renovating, Fels-Naptha soap stands far away above all other helps for ease, quickness and thoroughness.</p>
        <p>Consider what a difference 'o the tired mother where able to save about half the mere manual force required by the old way of washing clothes, as well as about half the time in doing it.</p>
        <p>Clothes come out whiter, sweeter and nicer. Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson (greet Lonsicin EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MESSRS. CROSFIELDS' NEW OFFICES.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELDS' NEW OFFICES.</p>
        <p>DINNER TO CELEBRATE THE OPENING.</p>
        <p>Interesting Records of the Firm's Early Days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD M P., MR. J J CROSFIELD AND OTHERS.</head>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD M P., MR. J J CROSFIELD AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>The handsome new offices erected by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons Limited at Bank Quay are rapidly nearing completion. The buildings are of imposing dimensions and are certainly an ornament to the town, as well as affording evidence of the increasing pros- perity of a great industry. All that now remains to be done is the interior decoration and furnishing, and on Monday evening occasion was taken to celebrate the formal openiag of the offices by the holding of a dinner. Invitations had been issued by the directors to the whole of the mem- bers of the staff and the function, which took place in the commolaius room which is to serve for general offices, was a highly successful and enjoyable one. Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P., presided and amongst those present were Mr. J. J. Crosfield, Captain CL It. Cros- field, Messrs. R. F. Jones,C. F. Huff= and H, Roberts (directors), A. Warburton (architect of the building), C. W. Davenport (builder), F. R. Roberts (solicitor to the company),J. R. Wardle (secretary to the company)' and Dr. J. Adams. Before the guests sat down to dinner a flash-light photogroph was taken by Birtles. An excellent dinner was served, the catering being in the hands of the Warrington Cafd Company. Afterwards the usual toast list was gone through and some interesting speeches were delivered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"THE KING."</head>
        <p>"THE KING."</p>
        <p>The CHA IRMAN, in proposing this toast, said it was always received in this country not only with respect but with cordial and sincere affection. He was sure there was nobody in that room who did not feel what splendid service to our country and to our empire had been rendered by the genial, dignified and tactful sovereign who was recognised to-clay as the first diplo- matist in Europe. He asked them to drink from their hearts and with all cordiality the toast of our beloved sovereign his Majesty King Edward VIE. The toast was drunk with great enthusias:n.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>"THE PIRA". A RECORD OF PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>"THE PIRA". A RECORD OF PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. M. LOMAS proposed the toast of "be firm of Joseph Crostleld and Sons, Ltd." At the outset, he took the opportunity of congratulating the Chairman most heartily upon his recent well-earned victory. (Applause.) He trusted Mr. Crosfield would have health and strength to fulfil the duties devolving upon him, and that he would retain the seat for the borough of , Warrington for mnny years. (Applause.) It was very gratifying to him as the oldest member of the staff to be called upon to propose the toast of " Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd.,"a name that had been well- esteemed from the time of his earliest recollections. , The firm, although it was closely verging upon its centenary, showed no signs of senile decay, but, on the contrary, was wonderfully energetic, progressive, and well upto-date. Its vitality wa^. so great that it was constantly increasing its responsibilities by adding new industries to its already lengthy list. Those new industries, coupled with the great ievelopment of the old ones, necessitated the erection of a vast amount of new plant and a pro- portionate increase in the number of employees. It was difficult even for him to realize the great expansion that had taken place, during his long service. From being a small, compact manufactory near the river side, the works had assumed gigantic proportions. The original boundaries were entirely lost and still the cry was : " More land for extension." He trusted that development would continae. (Applause.) He was : sure they would all agree that the directors bad spared no expense :n the erection of that beautiful building which was a great credit to them and an orna- ment to the town of Warrington. (Applause.) ' The toast was cordially honoured. PRESENTATION TO MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P. Mr. A. WARBURTON, the architect, then presented to Mr. A. H. Crostleld, M.P., a beautiful gold key as a memento of the interesting occasion. MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P. _ AND THE FIRM'S</p>
        <p>• • • PROGRESS. l(frr. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P., who had an enthusiastic reception, responded. Ho said there was an old French proverb qui s'excuste s'accase but he thought he must tender a little apology to them in the first instance cause he understood that the chair was going to be taken that evening by his brother who, with the com- prehensive and capable grasp he had of the business,was very much better qualified to occupy the chair than an unfortunate individual like himself (the speaker) who had been called to undertake duties in another place. (Laughter.) He was afraid their humorous reception of that apology did not quite convey the sympathy he had expected—(leighter)—in the arduous and unfortunate situatiou in which he was placed, but he did not want them to take it quite too literally. If he might say a word parenthetically, he was afraid they would think he was vorging on something that was notcommercial—(laughter)—but if he did so it was only because his old friend Mr. Lomas, the oldest mem- ber of the staff in their offices, had hi ,self transgressed the lines and alluded to a little b .tt: that was fought not so very long since in that ..ei&amp;hbourhood in which he (Mr. Orosfield) took an accive part. When Mr. Lomas was speaking he could not help thinking of some of his own earlier recollections of the firm which, taken together with what he had heard from his father, his uncles, and Mr. Lomas, must carry one back a long way. There were some stories in the Warrington press the other day which made him rub his eyes again. He found that the founder of that concern was com- pared to the Czar of Russia and he thought it was a very wonderful thing that that staid, stern old Quaker. Joseph Crosfleld,should be compared to the Czar—either the present poor weakling or any other Czar of Russia. (Hear, hear.) Then there had been anecdotes about the early days of the concern when they laboured under the evils or the blessings—(laughter)—of Protection— anecdotes connected with an uncle of his whom Mr. Lomas would well remember. He did not think he need dwell upon those at the present time because they had heard all about them quite recently. He would, how- ever, like to refer to the more recent times, and in the first place he was sorry that the whole of the directorate was toot with them that night. He was sure they would all endorse cordially the telegram which had been sent to Dr. lklarlel—(applause)—who unfortunately could not be present. With that exception the directors were there in full force. (Applause.) That occasion was certainly a very memorable one. It was a land- mark in the development of a great progressive firm. (Applause.) He had already alluded to some political matters and he dare say they had heard a great deal about Progressives lately. He was sure, however, that they would not accuse him of any political referende in expressing himself in that way, because they were progressives there—(hear,hear) and the firm had been progressive ever since the days of Joseph Crosfleld. (Applause). The respected founder of their concern said on one occasion that " heaven itself would not be heaven to him unless there w is pro- gress there." If he (Mr. Crosfield) were asked to sug- gest a motto for everyone connected with the concern, either on the technical or the commercial side he could not offer them anything better than that, and he was happy to think that beautiful building, which redounded so much to the credit, initiative and skill of Mr. Warburton and the excellent work which under his direction had been carried out by Mr. Davenport, was an evidence' and a very tangible one too, of the progress which the firm had made in the years gone by, even since he joined it,</p>
