 Crosfield, of Lancaster," givine the earliest history they During the evening an admirable musical programme had of Josep'i Crosfield, the rounder of the firm. The was submitted and all . the items were well first entry Is ein 1792 read: "At half-past one (p.m.) V received. Master H. Griffiths, who posseses my dear N cy presented us with nnother fine lad to the a beautifully sweet voice, sang " Angels ever great joy of us all. We mean to name him Joseph." In bright and faer " very expressively. Mr. Baxter 1812 the record stated : "On • son Joseph reached his was heard to advantage in" Let me love thee," parents' dwelling. He has been an apprentice with and Miss F. Leicester very effectively interpreted "My Anthony Clapham (chemist and druggist) nearly am n folk." Miss A. L. Brundrett was vvarmly applauded six years; he looks thin, is lively and for her rendering of" The year's at the spring." Mr. good tempered. He apr ears to possess a large IJ. R. Wardle gave" Will o' the wisp " with fine spirit share of good nature but still inclined to and another acceptable item was ' I fear no foe ' by offer his opinion on almost every subject mentioned." Mr. E. Morris. The duties of accompanist were ably (Laughter). In 1814 an entry read : "I left Lancaster discharged by Mr. H. Clayton. on my way to Warrington, rested at night at Yarrow Bridge. a comfortable house in a rural situation reached Warrington next day about 12. The object of ray journey was to view some premises near Bankey suitable for a soapery, which bu.iness our son Joseph seems to have a strong inclination to. I stopped at Warrington till the 15th, hut made no positive agree- ment." Later in the year the diary stated: "Son Joseph returned from Warrington having concluded a bargain for the premises at Sankey. He continued with us a week and then proceeded to Newcastle to obtain a knowledge of making soap." In 1815 there was an entry "%Vent to view and inspect our son Joseph's soap works at Bankey, which are very complete, but: the trade is a losing one." In 1817 the journal read : " Son Joseph appears in good spirits, his trade being much better of late " and then in 1818 " Joseph went on horseback across the sands to visit his love at Bayclifie. He appears in high spirits ' • his business has been profitable for about a Year. by which he has regained his previous heavy loss ' Other entries referred to son Joseph's marriage and in 1819 there was this item : "To War- . .</p>
