      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EMILY R. CARLING.</head>
        <p>EMILY R. CARLING.</p>
        <p>" 'Ali Sin was his name ; And I shall not deny • in regara to the same 'What that name might imply." So says Bret litarte : but what has the "heathen C'hinee " of whom he writes—or another of whom he does not write —to say on the subject? Has be no key to guide us ill our 1 wanderings along those curious twists and turns which go to make up his notion of the idea symbolised by the word Ski? Oh, yes, he has a key ; but can We of the West, who are so young and so impatient, hope to be accounted worthy to be entrusted with it 14 Let us try to prove ourselves so. If, then, we would use it aright, we must begin with an ap- preciation of the " Rules of Politeness." which have regulated Chinese conduct for over 2,450 years, and try to understand a Chinaman's con- ception of the duty of man to man, the violation of which is sin. The greatest of all sins in the eyes of a Confucianist, is disobedience or disrespect t• e Superiors. Superiors may be divided into various classes. All who are older than ourselves are our Superiors. Respect is due to the Emperor as "The Son of Heaven," to the parents who gave us birth and cared for us in infancy and child- hood, and to the teachers who have instructed us and prepared us to fight the battle 4 life nobly and win a good name. Hie ELDERS AND BETTERS. It is essential that a young man should at all times conduct himself with decorum in the pre- sence of his superiors. In addressing them he must not lift his eyes above the level of their waists. When he meets them in the street he must stand aside with his arms straight down while they pass by, or if he meets them for the first time in the year, he must bowdown before them till his head nearly touches the ground. Nor is this all: The obligations of Filial Piety include the Care of parents and aged relatives during their lifetime and of their spirits after death. Every Chinaman is supposed to possess three souls : one goes into the ground at burial, one into the Ancestral Tablet, and one into the realm of spirits. It is partly in order to provide the last with those things which are requisite to its well-being that Ancestor-worship is kept up, departed ancestors often taking revenge on the living for any negligence on their part in the performance of the cuNtomary rites for the dead by bringing some calamity upon the household. such as plague cholera or unfilial children. These " Rites for the Dead " are performed for all over 10 years of age, with the hope of delivering them from Hades or of improving their condition there. They last for a shorter or longer period according to the age and wealth of the deceased. For children and young people from seven days to a month, for a middle-aged man of ordinary means, from a month to days. Sometimes a day is reckoned for each year of life, 15 years old, 15 days, dm On the last day, a paper house, sedan-chair and paper servants are burnt, with a complete set of house- hold furniture. The house for a man contains a " study " furnished with writing and reckoning materials: that for a woman a boudoir' " with looking-glass, hair-comb, toilet powders and other requisites. Twice each day during the " Rites " food is offered, the essence of which passes into Hades in steam. After the comple- tion of the ‘,‘, Rites," food is offered twice • year, on the anniversaries of birth and death. DEBTS AND, BARGAINS. Such, then, are BORIC of the leading character- istics of a man's duty towards Ids Superiors hi both worlds. We will now pass on to consider his duty towards his equals. Of primary import- ance in this connection, is the duty to pay every man his due. All accounts must be settled be- fore dawn on New Year's Day. As may be ex- pected, the creditor is anxious to make he most of the vagueness of the term "dawn," and may be seen long after dawn on th ant day of the year, hurrying along the street, with a lantern in his hand, trying to persuade 'His Majesty the Sun, that it is yet night, and he . may law- fully pursue the unhappy debtor and secure his due. As likely as not he is on a wild-goose wild- g chase, for the pursued is probably' a bankrupt. In this case he will lose his money, but by far , the greater loss is that of the debtor, who has lost his " face " for ever. If there is one thing . dearer than life and dearer than cash to John, it is reputation. 'When that is gone, the life, which is no longer worth -living, often follows it. Many a man, unable to meet his liabilities at the end of the. Chinese year, has had re- course to tire rope, the river, or to poison. Dishonesty- is likewise condemned, but the Eastern standard of honesty differs somewhat from that of the West. For example, .when making a bargain, every man is entitled to do the best he can for himself, and 4f you are foolish enough to be taken in, you are the only one to blame for jour folly : the proceeding is perfectly honourable. Once the bargain is made, however, you 'may be sure it will be kept. On one occasion a price was given for the making of a bedstead with a rattan bottom : while the bedstead was in hand, the price of rattan went up. When it was brought horns, the carpenter said, with a smiling face. " You have the best of it this time, I did not know the price of rattan was going up when I gave you the estimate. I have lost over a dollar on that bedstead." A higher price was offered, but John was offended. "No," he said. "A bargain is a bargain and ray word is worth more than cash."</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL MORALITY. The cook, who buys your food, furnishes another example of " honesty." He is entitled by the rules of his profession—of which, by the way, you may be ignorant—to make a profit on every transaction ; that is to say, the shop- keeper allows him a certain discount, so that the probability is that in buying through your cook, you pay no more for an article than if you had brought it from the shop-keeper : perhaps less, for the cook knows better than you do the market price of the article in question. Theft is very severely dealt With : even for a slight offence the thief may be suspended from 11 tree by his hair for a day or more, during which time he is a target at which the boys will be likely to throw stones. Loose language is more or less permissible, but he who is discovered lying feels it to be a disgrace, as it proves him to be inefficient in the art. Murder in a good cause is justifiable, though punishment is inevitable for the murderer, and, as a rule, life must be given for life. In cases of suicide, if the cause can be traced to an ag- gravating party, he will be counted guilty of murder.</p>
        <p>AS A PARENT.</p>
        <p>Again we must pass on to consider a man's duty to his inferiors. Opium-smoking, im- morality and gambling are discountenanced and parents are earnest in,: their endeavours to save their children from these evils. Here is an in- stance of the way in which they try to do this. A woman whose husband is an opium-smoker toils hard to keep the home together. " I have done all that a woman can do," she says, " but my husband goes from bad to worse." She cannot help the cook to catch a foul which he is aboLlt to Drevoitt for dinner. beutuse tu</p>
        <p>vegetarian ana wilt not mann in ..—ae taking o- life. "And why - are you a vegetarian f' we ask. "Oh," she says, In order that Heaven may preserve my son from opium." A father, however, has not discharged his duty to his son until he has educated, appren- ticed and married him. After he has done so, he is not likely to sin grievously in his treatment of his inferiors. Thus we may say that apart' from the satisfactory discharge of the duty of a man to his superiors, equals and inferiors, John knows little of the mowing of the word Sin. It is rarely that a troubled conscience clouds the hrow or the mnii in the street, or dlsturbs hie well-enreted sltunbers.</p>
        <p>Industrious. thrifty, patient. ' practical, re- sourceful John ! Why should he carry a heavy • heart, or murmur at what Heaven decrees f " IVhatever is, is best " for .Tohn. He is a philosopher in his way. and lives a life wonder- fully free from anxious thought. Yet there are those who know something of unrest, and who seek to find a way to the attain- ment of the highest bliss. This is seen in those Buddhist Monasteries which are scattered over the mountains in just those spots " where every prospect pleases." I I ere the would-be saint retires from the world, that he may meditate on the Universe and wor- ship in sincerity those gods and heroes of old, among whom he hopes one day to find a place. He does not drink intoxicants, nor smoke, nor gamble • be is it strict veeetarian and will on no account destroy life. some will even torture themselves to death, with the hope that the highest places in bliss may be theirs. Here is a monastery with its 500 resident priests, each of whom bears the nine scars of his ordination on his clean-shaven head. What he must have suffered as those sticky cones were left to smoulder on his brow I A courteous welcome is given to all visitors, and we are free to inspect the grounds, to admire the ancient trophies of the monastery, to watch the priests dining in silence in their spotless dining-ball, or hear them recite their prayers, to the monotonous boom of the accompanying drum. • MB RELIGION. But td return once more to the masses, in times of national calamity there seems to be some kind of awakening to a sense of sin apart from disobedience to the Rules of Politeness which we have discussed. At such times it is thought that evil spirits may have been unwit- tingly offended. _ .</p>
        <p>. thus during a 844, no life is taken, in order that these spirits may be appeased. Again, in times of drought, it is thought that Heaven is offended. The streets of the city are thronged with the neighbouring villagers, who join with the city men—for what man in China has no part nor lot in the fields of his ancestors ? Idols are brought from the temples and shrines and carried through the streets cf the city and round the, fields, that they may see the state of the crops( and the misery of the people and carry a report to Heaven. The procession is headed by kneeling priests, clothed in sack- cloth, and as it moves slowly along, the cry ascends from every lip " Oh Heaven, send rain !" "Oh Heaven, send rain !" And if there is no rain? . . . Then it is ordained s that John should travel to the Unseen by the long road of starvation instead of by the short road of pesti- lence.</p>
        <p>And here we must deliver up the key which has thus far aided us in eur investigation of some of the rules by which John lives and dies, as seen in the outward manifestations of life.</p>
        <p>But of that inner life, of whose depths none but John can know, it is beyond our power to speak, yet we are persuaded that at times even the " Heathen Chinee " must know something of what Dr. IL J. Druminond calls that " gulf between what .umu is and what he ought to be."</p>
        <p>My Dear Children, I have sent off no less than twelve of my post- card albums this week to those who have sent me in lists of twenty-four new names tor the Children's Corner League, we now have about I,tocr mem- bers, and I shall be glad to have as many more as possible. In future all those who Wish to send me in these lists must send me atstaMped addressed envelope for the proper forms. I can- not accept any lists of new members unless sent in on these sheets that I supply. Below you will see a very interesting letter from Alice Lathan, I did not print the poetry as I had no room, but perhaps it will appear a little later in these columns. I have received contri- butions from William Clarke, Barry E. Clarke, Nancy Porter, and Gladys Kendall, all these will be Published later and prizes duly awarded, but .this week I have no space left. Gertie Monk must try again and remember to Write on one side of the paper, and I hope one day to be able to award her a prize. lam sending_ prizes this week to J. Richardson and Sydney Elliot for the contributions printed below, I hope that these members will continue to send in -these shirt stories and pieces of poetry. I expect you are all busy now with our monthly competitions, the boys with the handwriting and the girls dressing dolls. Remember that these, latter will be given to hospitals orio some charity, in each case three handsome prizes will be awarded. With all good wishes from</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned, - I writ i to thank yoo very much for the nice postcard album, also for the nice blotting book that I received at Christmas. I think it is very kind of you to give such nice presents to your nephews and neices. I am very glad to be a member of the Children's Corner. I think it a nice thing for all the boys and girls to be mem- bers of the Children's Corner, if they try and be kind to each other and to dumb animals, and feed the poor birds this &amp;old weather.</p>
        <p>I will finish by sending you porno poetry called</p>
        <p>, MY LESSON. I used to kill birds in my boyhood, ' Blackbirds and robins and wrens; - I hunted them up on the hillsides, I hunted them down in the glens. I never thought is was sinful, I did it only for fun; I had rare sport in the forest, • With the poor little birds and my gun. But one clear day in the springtime I spied a brown bird in the tree, Merrily swinging and chirping, As happy as bird could be. - And raising my gun in a twinkling I fired, but my aim was too true, For a moment the little thing fluttered \ - Then off to the bushes it flew. ' I followed it quickly and softly, - And there, to my sorrow I found— Right close to4ts nest of young ones— _ • : The little bird dead on the ground. Poor birdies for food they were calling, But now they could never be fed, For the kind mother bird who had loved them • , - Was lying there, bleeding and dead. • I picked up the bird in my anguish And stroked the wee motherly thing, That nevermore could feed her dear young ones. Nor dart through the air on swift wing. And I made a firm vow in that moment Tildia Vt never my heart with such sorrow was stirred, ever again in my lifetime Would I shoofa poor innocent bird. From JOSEPH RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned, I received my HON7S' Blotter a few days ago, I think it is very nice. lam sending you a story i and I hope you will like it. It s called "A Strange Nurse." A spaniel belonging to a farmer had some pups, of which she took great care. If scraps of food were .given her, she always went to share the gift with her young ones. The farmer was corysitlerably puzzled to understand how bones kept being put into the tiaby's cradle, and one day the child's mother resolved to watch. She was sitting by the fire when the -baby began to cry. Instantly the spaniel came running into the linuse with a bone in her mouth, which she tried to put into baby's mouth. She seemed to think.the baby wanted something to eat, and as' a bone satisfied her babies, and kept-them quiet, she evidently concluded that the baby in the Cradle could be pleased in a similar way. num your loving nephew,. SYDNEY ELLIOTT.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned, hope you will like the story I am sending you. It is called</p>
        <p>A LION HUNT, It was while I was exploring in Central Africa with my friehd Jack Towers that the following adventure befell me. I was sitting in my veran- dah, enjoying a cool smoke, when a. crowd of natives from the next village came running thrdrigh, and in the midst of them a youlig suspended by the teet to a roughly hewn bough, and being, carried along by. two huge blacks. Two of the foremost who had been benefited by</p>
        <p>NIANES "Ch a ro. ct e nsed by an excellent rtch proportion of nitrogenous loud 'ubst“nces. "—THE LANC ET o Years Estabhshed Reputatioa-</p>
