      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HIGHER EDUCATION IN CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>HIGHER EDUCATION IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Costly Schemes.</p>
        <p>• A meeting of the Cheshire Education Committee was held on Monday at Crewe. The members discussed the question of the provision and maintenance of secondary schools and other forms of higher education in the county. The committee recommended that county secondary schools should be provided at Altrincham (for 200 pupils), Wallasey (for 300 boys). Wallasey (for 250 girls), Macclesfield (for 220 girls), Hyde (250), and Crewe (350 pupils). The estimated cost of the buildings would he £4O per pupil, or altogether .£62,800 for the six schools mentioned. A num- ber of schools already existed, and as far as possible these would be utilised for the benefit of the county. The estimated cost of mainten- ance of secondary schools in future years, when all suggested were built and full, would be about £7.000 a year. The committee also recommended that " higher elementsry schools" should be 'established a Altrin- chant, Hoylake, 13ebbington and Neston district, and Northwich and Wilmslow district ; that the portion of capital expenditure incurred on acconntof the provision of any higher elementary school (to be charged on the district served by the school) should bp one-half the cost of such expenditure; and the commitee thouqht It might be desirable to convert certain existing secondary schools into higher elementary schools.</p>
        <p>THE BLACK SMOKE DIFFICULTY.. At a meeting of the St. Helens Health Com-sittee on Wednesday, Dr. Buchan, medical officer, submitted a long report on the question of black smoke, ant stated that there were 185 works' chimneys in the town emitt- ing smoke. In one case the chimney had to deal with thirty-six fires and furnaces. During the last year 396 observations were made, snd in sixty cases the limit of five minutes was exceeded. Full details of the various chimneys and works were given, and the powers of the local authority discussed.—The Chairman inquired what happened at the recent Conference on Black Smoke.— Alderman J. C. Gamble said that absolutely nothing was done. All the old theories were discussed, just the same as twenty years ago.—On the suggestion of the Chair- man the committee decided to consider the report, and deal with it at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
