        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>iy skill as a doctor, rushed up to me and i formed me by means of signs and their roui English lingo that the parents of this lion 1L created such havoc in their village by carrying I two of their tellow natives, and that they vvishi me to join in the hunt, which was going to tal place in the afternoon. Accordingly we set c in the afternoon accompanied by a crowd blacks. We had been following the trail</p>
        <p>Congregational Gathering.</p>
        <p>- ,of The annual congregational gathering in connection of a, with St. Luke's Church was held in the schoolroom, lion for about an hour, when we were suddenly Bank Quay, on Monday' evening and was largely attended startled by a rough rustling from a tree behind the room being crowded in every part. An excellent tea us. All of us immediately turned round, just in was provided after which an admirable entertainment ' several time to see a great lion spring out of the thicket. was held, the programme being submitted by The nearest person to it was the chief of the members of the congregation and friends. All the items natives, and his wife was by his side. The lion were well received, most of them being enthusiastically encored. The usual votes of thanks were accorded. was about to give an enormous spring when a shot from one of the blacks pierced its leg, and " Why We Believe." it fell backwards on to the tree. It began to roar The Rev. Father Edward 0.5.F.C., gave the second very loudly. Another shot from me soon put'an end lof his series of sermons on "Faith," at. St. Mary's to its sufferings, and we returned home with (R.C.) Church, Latchford, on Sunday,dealing with "Why the lion. The blacks said that it was the same we believe." He took as his text," It is impossible for lion which had created the havoc. After this I ' God to lie " (St: Pant's epistle to the Hebrews vi. 18), returned to England, but I am always thinking i The rev,_ father dealt with the criticism levelled at about this adventure. Christian believe, and what it was based upon, and con- From your loving nephew, trasted it with the greatness and infallibility of God the creator of all things. Learned scientists, he con- BERTIE BAMFORD. tinned, did not accept some things connected with the faith because "they could not understand them, or reason them out." How untenable was that position, seeing that these same scientists, who would not aceept and believe in the mysteries of God, proved their incon- sistency by believing the mysteries of nature, which were the handiwork of God. Was it not unreasonable to say they believed in nature's mysteries, but not in their Author—the great and infallible Creator of all things? The faith had been attacked, and would again be attacked, but God would keep it triumphant. The New Parliament. Sermon by the Rev. H. Dunnlco. The Baptist Church was crowded in all parts on Sun- day last, many having to be accommodated in the aisles and vestibules. The Rev. H. Dunnico, who occupied the pulpit,made special reference to the now Parliament to be opened on Tuesday. Basing his remarks on Deuteronomy xxxii. 7," Remember the days of old,con- sider the years of many generations," the preacher said it seemed to him that this was precisely the kind of appeal most necessary at the present time. The study of history was little pursued In these modern days. The newspaper with its passing events, forgotten as soon as read, the magazine with tiny crumbs of information and much that was superficial, the novel light and trivial were widely read, but few made any attempt to study the history of their country. These and other causes I too numerous to mention nad resulted in a neglect of all serious reading. Yet every wise thinker and teacher clearly saw that a nation that scorned to notice the lessons of the past inevitably drifted into ruin. We were undoubtedly living in swage of unbridled sport ! and pleasure, and history revealed that the very same ' feature brought the mighty Roman Empire to the dust. To-day we saw a vast accumulation of wealth In a few 1 hands. The rich were becoming richer, the poor _poorer, the growth of trusts tended to increase tyranny. History revealed that there was a limit to human endurance and that once that limit was passed the flames of revolu- tion followed. On Tuesday next Parliament would assemble, and whatever their politics might be they all sincerely trusted that the men composing it would be GRANDMA'S STORY. led to do all things with no other motive than the best interests of their great Empire. In the heat and fervour (Concluded.) of a general election they could not avoid becomiag strong partizans, but now the fight was over they knew "'She. really did love me then, if she no parties save that they were all opposed to social tried to save me,' I said to myself, and sank injustice and political wrong wherever it might exist. down on the road and cried. I don't know llt was in that spirit that he appealed to all, especially how long, but I heard a shout; which made , yonng men, to Remember the days of old." Their beloved Empire had grown in a few generationsfrom. me look up, and there was a crowd of people a mere speck in the ocean until to-day it round me. I heard them say to a police- : comprised one fourth of the whole wide world. man, Here is Miss White's niece, quite INo nation had a more glorious heritage, and if they safe." Of all the miracles! Where have were not degenerate sons they could not do other than you been? We thought you were burnt,' !bare their heads and tread with humble reverence the the policeman said. I was not there. I left • hallowed grousd of ther birth. To obtain and preservei that heritage their fathers had fought, suffered cruel 'my room at five o'clock. I ran away, and mockery, died at the stake and for generations the road slept in the cave until half an hour agb.' of liberty was lit by the blaze of martyrs' fires. Surely I sobbed out. Well, did you leave a lint lif they remembered these things they would be worthy burning? ' questioned a fireman. Yes, II of them. He appealed to the young men to fit them- answered. I felt as if I could confess any. selves for a noble life by studying the past for a little thing then, while, to do their part well, to be true to those faiths " Somebody took me to their house, and and velours by which their fathers had conquered because if they. failed, if they were indifferent to noble' later on my father and mother came to I ideals the world would ere long witness the fall of a fetch me. I could hardly face them, but I greater empire than that of acient Rome. told them all about it. My mother did not cal- anything to me about it then, but in Sermon by the Rev. John Yonge. the train, two days after, when we were Preaching at Wycliffe Church, on Sunday evening,the therefore, i s thi of f remarks going home, she said: I know that your , Rev. John Youge took for his subject " Prayer for the up iocna itihoe n s words" prayers I exhor duntie was rather cross sometimes, but she; "2•, "P rat m ent, , 13f has had a lot of trouble, which I will tell , intercessions and giving of thank'ss bePma de for all men, ' you about another day. I thin , ,k you have for iK. ngs,and for all that are in authority, that we may had your punishment, dear,' she added aria lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and 11 voEr ' honesty "—I Tim. i. 1 and 2 After explaining I hope it will be a lesson to you a , c. the ircurastance s under which these words were written, life not to be ha.t2.-.' " • • there bad, said the preacher, been Christian people who "Did you never see your aunt again: ;had deprecated the introduction, into public prayer of asked Ruth. " Xo, dears, I did not. I am what they deemed secular subjects, even as they con- telling you this story that it may be a lesson sidered public national affairs to be outside the domain to You all your life; as it has been to me." of Christian activity ; and it might, therefore, do the • GLADYS M. KENDALL. , congregation good that evening to be remiuded that Church d bChurch ht t !player in thean y theought otake within its scope all things which affected the welfare of mankind. The question arose" Are we justified in regarding the result et the election as in any sense an buswer to Christian prayer ?" Looked at from the standpoint of the Free Churches of the country they might be perfectly ready so to regard it, as thel election had eventuated in a way that had wrought astonishment even in the most sanguine, and the profoundest gratification throughout at any rate the great bulk of Nonconformists. But in what many of them might regard as the day of a signal triumph, and as a time full of 'blight hope for the advancement of civil and religious liberty, they must not forget that to very many others, even to many who were as truly ieligious and patriotic as themselves, the result of the conflict was quite the reverse It was not only a party defeat but it was, in their belief, a national and a religious disaster. There wore large numbs rs of sincere Christians who deplored the electoral Jesuit as one which struck at the root of religion in the schools. They were, therefore,honestly.trembling for the Ark of God. TheY differed from them, but did it not become them to be generous and magnanimous? and the fact- that in the past they might have failed to exhibit these Christian graces was all the greater reason why they (those present and other congregations) should not fail. They said they did notibelieve in a policy of retaliation. In his judgment there ought to ho a frank readiness to confer. concede and conciliate on the teaching of religion in the schools of the State, if only the greet fundamental ,princi:sles of entirely equal religious liberty,and irpublic ;raoney not being used for sectarian ends be established and safeguarded. The resolution shown in the General 'Election not only ushered in a new and better era in 'national history, but was the workin,, out of the Provi- dence of God, and it could not be at all wondered at that the Free Churches should be jubilant and grateful. The fact that the new Parliament contained close upon 180 Nonconformist members was not only unparalleled in history, but was replete with hope and promise. Most, if not all, of these men had been prominent, power- ful and devoted in Christian services in Christian Churches. They bad in many instances been allied with the masses instead of the classes, and understood the needs and aspirations of the people. Many of them were, or had sprung from the ranks of working men, and, entering Parliament as Labour members, they wauld be loyal to the State in being loyal to their own order. He had complete confidence in the ihtegrity and the patriotism of the Labour representation, and there was no need to fear for any righteous cause in their hands. No British cause could be more urgent than the welfare and social salvation of the poor, and th.at cause could only be promoted in and by the Parliament of the nation. ,It had clamouted by petition and deputa- tion long enough, and now it would be able to speak and to promote necessary legislation. It was their duty to pray for the new Parliament. They had the happi- ness of knowing that in it, more, perliaps, than in any</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned, _ I was so glad when I saw my story in print. But I was simply delighted when I knew I was entitled to a prize. I seat a letter with my story and poetry, to tell you they were original, and the address was in- side. lam afraid you did not get it. Had you not space to put in the poetry, or did you not get it? I have written it out again in case the other was mislaid. I would like to see it in the "Cor- ner" next week if _there is space. .</p>
        <p>I am interested in the "Children's Corner," and I like. to read tlle stories. _</p>
        <p>I think that poetry called "Father's coming home" was very pretty indeed, and beautifully worded. F wonder if it was original. Once I began to " edit" a magazine, amongst my school friends.. It was a grand success. The school girls and boys in my class contributed and were the readers. The boys drew pictures for us. \ The girls had more imagination, for they wrote stories and poetry and painted pictures. I made rules, tnd they had to be kept. I was very busy in my spare moments, writing the serials, the corres- pondence column, advertisement column, and my editorial "chit-chat." Then we had competitions for stories, essays and other kinds. We used to assemble in a class room to read it.</p>
        <p>We used to have such flin,-a-na ;general "o,h" of dismay would go round when the bell rang for lessons. I must close now. Yours very sincerely, ALICE LATHAN.</p>
        <p>s- :</p>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</p>
