      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Dinner at the Town Pall. ---</head>
        <p>Dinner at the Town Pall. ---</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 17, 1906.-</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT responded and said he thought the committee,which had barely been in existence three years, had done a very good work indeed. They were very fortunate in their start, because the committee commenced under the chairmanship of Mr. Monks for whom they all entertained the highest respect, and was pioneered by Mr. Moore Murray the able and courteous director. (Applause.) He thought they had accom' plished work of which they were entitled to be proud. They had certainly done a great deal to improve the staffing of the elementary schools and had introduced enormous improvements in the management. They had established a secondary school which, in spite of the staircase which seemed still to worry the Mayor.— (laughter)—was a worthy institution, and they had brought the Technical Institute to a degree of efficiency which he thought was altogether beyond what it had achieved in the past. That day they had taken a further step forward by asking the Mayor to lay the foundation stone of the first new school which it had been neces- sary to build in order to meet the requirements of the Board of Education. Alderman Bennett commented upon the great work of Mr. Beamont, whose name would always be associated with the school, referring particu- larly to his antiquarian researches. He also expressed the view that the fact that the children would pass down O'Leary-street on the way to the school would be a permanent incentive to the spirit of patriotism which was not by any means the least of the qualities to be in- culcated by the educational system. Having touched upon the vast importance of education generally, Alderman Bennett said he did not know how long the Committee would be allowed to continue their good work. Their regime might be over in a' few months, and they might see the introduction of an entirely new system. He felt confident, however, that all the Committee had done had been animated by an honest desire to do the best they could for the town compatible with a spirit of reasonable economy, and according to the information and abilities which they possessed. (Applause)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>"THE MAYOR."</head>
        <p>"THE MAYOR."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon STEVENSON- proposed the health of "The Mayor." His Worship, he said, threw himself heart and soul into the work of his office and he (the speaker) fancied that if the present Government in its wisdom carried an eight hours' bill, the Mayor of War- rington would be one of the most grateful people in I England. (Laughter.) .A.l Chairman of the Sites and Buildings Committee his Worship had not spared himself and it had been his endeavour to see the work done as efficiently as possible. Canon Stevenson concluded by saying that the Mayor's hospitality was known and recognized by all and his genial spirit and friendly con- verse made him a princely entertainer. (Applause.) The Maxon responded. Referring to educational matters be said it was ,first intended to carry on the work in Warrington for a rate of sd. in the £, but the rate now stood at is. and he did not know whether it would not reach is. 6d. He pointed out that the new school had been built strictly in accordance with the directions of the Board of Education on the most up- to-date-lines and the expense was therefore unavoid- able. He had lone his best to prevent all extravagant expenditure. Nothing bad yet been put on the rates for nes schools but if the Board of Education insisted on new schools being built in all parts of the district the rates would soon, be affected. "THE ARCHITECT." -</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB proposed this toast. He said he had known Mr. Silcock from boyhood and it had always been a pleasure to know him. He had every confidence that the new school would be a credit to the architect and to the town. (Applause). In responding Mr. SILCOCK said he was sorry that that afternoon's ceremony had not been the opening, instead of the laying of the foundation stone. As far as his care and attention were concerned, he would see that the work did not suffer. He was very pleased indeed to have the co-operation of Mr. Davenport and Mr. Hallard. (Aplause). "THE BUILDER."</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST proposed the health of " The builder "and expressed great Pleasure that Mr. Daven- port had recovered from his illness and was able to undertake such an important scheme. He hoped Mr. Davenport would live long to give his skill and ability for the benefit of the borough. (Applause). MR. DAVENPORT AND TETE NON-TRADERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. DIVENPORT briefly responded. He said he did not wish to be personal to anyone but he was convinced that whatever promises he made would be by one or two of his spacial friends in the Council perhaps multi- plied by two. He therefore refrained from m tking any promises. His experience of the Council in municipal buildings had been very considerable and of late years his recollections had been most pleasant, not from the fact that he had ever had anything given to him—he had never sought for it. He had always endeavoured and so far had carried out the buildings according to the contract as signed. The same feeling did not seem I to have existed during the last few years on the part ' of a certain section of the Council—one or more in particular. He was sorry this thing should occur, but nevertheless it was a fact. He was referring now to what were termed non-traders, but he should say the cheese-paring or false economy party. (Cries of "No, no," and "Yes, it's quite right.") He had never had the opportunity before of retaliating, although he did not wish to retaliate in any way, but Ihe had never had the ooportnnity of ewounding his views before the Council. He thought it would have been better if he bad been asked to come before the Committees on different occasions. He could mention for example one of the buildings he had bad to do with—the police buildings. They would remember very well that it was between six and seven years since the building was commenced. During that time he had made three attempts to complete the contract and so far he had been unsuccessful. There was no doubt that when the work was completed, which he hoped would be very soon, he would consider that he had not been treated in a straightforward manner by certain people in the Council —and one in particular. The MAvort : I would say nothing about that. We are a happy family well met to-night. Mr. DAVENPORT: Why I mention false economy in this— The MAYOR Let it lie. Mr. DAVENPORT: I believe I Rm bound to say this—</p>
        <p>--- The MANOR. : are here to proppse your hcalth and we want to drink it. . . _ _</p>
        <p>M. DANENrogT : When the building is completed I shall be able to prove to myself that thera has been false economy in the building of the police station. The time it ought to have been completed and the time it will be completed will be very far apart. During the evening songs were rendered by Mr. R. T. Fairclough and Mr. T. May gave a recitation. Mr. H. Mallard gave several humorous items in excellent style. An enjoyable gathering closed with the singing of 'Acid Lang Syne " andthe National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
