      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Lazy Workhouse Inmate.</head>
        <p>Lazy Workhouse Inmate.</p>
        <p>Henry Palmer, a pauper in the Warrington Work- house, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday with refusing ta parform his allotted task He was given 31bs. of oakum to pick, but a little over lib. was all he could manage. When visited by the porter he was found comfortably stretched on his back on a from, reading a book.— He was sent to gaol for six weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>It was only to be expected that Warrinton would obtain the maximum number of points at Millom, but when threequarter time approached with the score sheet blank a momentary shock was experienced in the breasts of the Wirepullers' supporters. From the start Warrington held the upper hand and time after time took up a strong position in the home quarters only to meet with an opposition so stubborn and determined as to render their efforts to score utterly futile. It was not until the later stays of the game had been reached that the Warrington forwards asserted themselves and taking matters into their own hands placed the issue of the match beyond doubt. One factor which contributed largely to the initial non-success of the Warrington team was an injury to Harris, that player having to leave the field with a damaged leg shortly after the commencement of hostilities.</p>
        <p>To the forwards alone is due the credit for the victory. Led by Preston:they worked with a will, and by superior tactics in the loose turned the tables on their opponents. In the scrum they were often beaten for possession—though they were fully able to hold the Millom eight, —through poor heeling. Preston was the pick and set excellent example. Early on he charged down a kick and quick to take advantage of the openinr, thus offered crossed the line only for the point to be disallowed. His recorded try was obtained by the wheeling of the pack near the Milloin line. The second try was the result of a purely forward move- ment the ball being dribbled nearly three -parts of the length of the field.</p>
        <p>At half-back Hockenhall made a welcome re-appear- ance and showed that he has lost none of his skill during his enforced absence. His return should mean all the difference between victory and defeat for War- rington. Lees, his partner, also played well but suffered by comporison with 019 comity man. The three-quarter lane can be dismissed in a sentence. Their tackling was good but their combination and passing weak. Berry was the " star " artist of the team and his coolness would have done credit to a much older bead. He had plenty of work to do and hardly made a mistake throughout the game.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men have a stiffer task on hand to-day when they tackle Kingston lovers at Hull. This match was on the fixture list for April 28th but both clubs having a vacant date it was re-arranged for this week. To the best of my recollections Warrington have never beaten the lovers at Craven- street, but they have been so near as a penalty goal. On October 9th last the lovers won at Wilderspool, so that Warrington have every reason for putting forth a strong effort in to-day's encounter. The rcatch should arouse considerable interest in Hull, seeing that the teams were contestants in the Cup final last April. Warrington's team will hardly be recognisable to those who witnessed the Headingley match, for Kenyon is the only one of the backs chosen to-clay who played in that historic encounter. The forwards are almost identical.</p>
        <p>Warrington's team will be : —Berry, full back; Kenyon, Jones; Taylor and Widdeson, three-quarters; Hockenhall and Lees, half-backs; Boardman, Preston, Shugars, Thomas, Belton, Harmer, Heath and Naylor, forwards. With such a side as this, though so differ- ent fror that of last season, there should be a fair chance of success, and I am in hopes of Warrington repeating their Salford performance.</p>
        <p>The "A " team, which had an easy win by 10 points to nil against Cadishead last Saturday, should not be -very seriously extended by Heyside to-day. The Morecambe lads are pretty new to the game, and they will find the following strong side opposed to them: -Knight, full-back ; Jordan, Dickinson, Cart- wright and Hewitt, threequarters; Lloyd and Garside; 'halves ; Forster, Morrison, Jolly, Lunt and Bradshaw, forwards.</p>
        <p>Harris has joined Warrington's injured list this week, but his damaged leg should not trouble him long. Isherwooctis likely to be fit for the Cup-ties and just now he is looking in the pink of health. Poor " Jack " Fish is still hobbling about on crutches, and I fear we shall hare to wait till next season ere there is any chance of seeing him once more bamboozling opponents and scoring tries as of yore. Dickinson showed much of his old form on Saturday, but another week in the "A " team should do him good.</p>
