        <p>The South-West Lineashire League up-to-date is as follows : Points.</p>
        <p>P. W.. • L. D. For At. Pts. Wisan Leigh 117,1rringt9n '7 . 1 .3 . 0.. 4'2— 23 ... 8 Runcorn Widnes S . 2 ... s .., 0,...19-59... 4 S. Helen - 49 ... 2 --7 Walker's • Brewery have a vefy interesting match or to-day, as thev. receive a' visit from Broughton!, Rangers "A" team, and there should be, a, good crowd on the Padgate.home ground. ' Th 9 first meet- ing of the two teams at Broughton prodnced an excel- lent game, and strong sides have been chosen for to-day.</p>
        <p>The Warrington ba•cks had better look to their laurels or they will have a worse ,scoring record than the forwards. In the last two matches,.the latter, by their dishing play in the loose, have been vesponsible for 25 points out of the ',Ft points scored. On Satur- day they secured both the tries. The .work of the backs was ahnost entirely of a defensive . character. Where, oh where is the combination? ( • The draw for the N.C.. Cup ' will take place on Monday in Manchester.</p>
        <p>• Wycliffe P.S.A. 'There was a very large gathering at the open meeting of this society on Sunday last. Dr. Ferguson of War- rington presided, and expressed himself delighted in having the honour of presiding over that grand meeting. His message to them was " Follow Christ." Mr. W. Bee Cher Smith of Manchester was. th 6 speaker, and spoke upon "The touch that adorns." There were .;he said three touches by Christ, "touch of language," "touch of nature" and "touch of character." The Warrington Apollo Male Voice Prize Choir, 60 voices, were present, and rendered` three selectic iis." Remember me," ,'Comrades" and. "The days Tarewell." Mr. Greenhough sang, "The Sailors Grace"; Mr. Gittens sang, " Nearer my God to Thee." The choir and the Soloists Were in splendid form and delighted the meeting. Mr. Harry Berrey conducted the choir and Mr. \V. H. Payton Mus. Bac., presided at the organ. Rev. John Yonge read the lesson and led the prayer, Tie also welcomed one new member. A good collection was taken for the funds of the Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR CONVENTION.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL GATHERING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Christian Endeavourers and Public Life.</p>
        <p>The annual convention in connection with the Warrington and District Union of Christian En- deavour Societies was held on Saturday at the Work- ingmen's Mission, Thewlis-street, and was in every way a success. There was a good attendance at the after- noon meeting which was under the chairmanship of the Rev. C. L. Stowe. _ _ -</p>
        <p>An interesting paper was presented by Miss M. E. NEWTON, Earlesto wn, on Christian Endeavour Comrade-</p>
        <p>ship. At no time, she said, had we so many societies and methods for bringing individuals into closer union with each other as at present and amongst the principal bodies formed with that object there was the Society--n movement in which people were more I closely banded together in Christian fellowship and were thereby able to present a stronger and more , united front to the forces of evil. In the Christian Endeavour movement there were splendid and unique possibilities of service for God. They had not yet realized their strength. Many claims were made upon them, and if they responded to the more pressing of those appeals they could do much to help the country by combating the evils of strong drink and gambling. Miss Newton instanced other directions in which all ' could help in the work, such as sick visitation, home mission army work, and house to house visitation. In so far, she thought, as they were united amongst them- selves would they be successful in their work, and the difficulties and discouragements which faced thorn would jbe overcome. (Applause). I Mr. H. GRAY presented a paper on "Christian j Endeavour and its hidden power." Close inquiry, he I said, into the constitution of the Christian Endeavour Society, would reveal that there was contained within it marvellous possibilities. To obtain the ideal in every industry,continued Mr. Gray,it was absolutely necessary that every man) should fill such position as his native powers entitled him to. Dr. Clarkle had an object similar to that in view when he founded the Christian Endeavour movement as in that Society opportunity was afforded for the development of many-sided talents, and full scope was given to the activities of every Endeavourer. (Applause). "The Christian Endeavourer's attitude towards public life " formed the subject of an interesting address delivered by the Rev. J. H. ROLLASON, of Earlestown. The object of the Society was, as far as they could, to make the earth have in it a little more of Heaven. If that was their object there was every reason why they should take a very active part in the public concerns with which they were surrounded. (Hear, hear.) There were men whose forte lay in the Church and in Church work and others whose whole efforts seemed to him naturally with engagements peculiar to public life. While one man fulfilled one part and one , another the two were employed upon a truly Christian I work( just as much as if both spent their whole lives engaged upon those things which were naturally associated with public worship. To house the poor under sanitary and healthy conditions and with an abundant supply of fresh air was as much a part of religion as to garnish the soul and make it clean. The only difference lay in degree and to be a Christian to him meant to embrace every department which affected the human life and to his mind there was comparatively as much religion in serving the community faithfully as there was to engage in a prayer meeting, in fact he was prepared to go so far as to say that he had , known conditions where the one had been really more expressive about religion than the other. The two could not be separated. If they were looking for some miracle—for some Divine interposition—in the hope that in some mysterious way sin would be taken out of the world and the unhealthy and unholy conditions which existed would be removed they were much mis- taken. They all prayed that this should come about but if they only prayed they could not expect their supplications to he answered as prayer meant work— work with this object in view. (Applause). A free parliament, in which several members took part, followed by the pronouncing of the benediction brought the afternoon proceedings to a close. During the afternoon Miss A. Woodland sang with effect " The wondrous Cross." The visitors to the convention were -invited to tea many availing themselves of the opportunity afforded, an excellent repast being provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EVENING MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE EVENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Evils of Militarism.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the evening meeting when Mr. W. T. Moss (presidens of the Union) presided and urged the members to take a greater interest in Christian Endeavour work. The principal speakers were the Rev. F. Hibbert, of Blackburn, and the Rev. H. J. Taylor, of Liverpool. In the course of a powerful address the Rev. F Hibbert said the one note that should be struck in con- nection with their work as Christian Endeavourers and as Christians was the note of reality. It was easy enough to profess to be a Christian and to be a member of a Christian Endeavour Society and it was not difficult to some people to join themselves with a Christian church ; but to be a real Endeavourer and a disciple of Jesus Christ was something far different and mach more difficult. What they wanted to make in their societies was not merely Christian professors and worshippers, but Christian men and women who were showing their Christian characters in their daily lives. There might be room in the Church for Chris- tians who were a little bit anemic and not quite so robust as they ought to be—for there was always the opportunity for improvement—but it always seemed to him that a Christian Endeavour Society, being mainly a society of young people, ought to be a society of Christians who were very much alive. He hoped the societies in the Warrington Districli, would have n splendid effect upon their members in making them lively,earnest, and robust. They were engaged in those societies in the great work of building up Christhan character—the greatest and most difficult work in the world. St. Paul told them "not to think more highly of themselves than they ought to think." Really it was impossible for them to think too highly of themselves. Ho (the speaker) was sore there were mere people who failed in life from the fact that they did not think sufficiently well of themselvea than from any other cause. There wore shoals of people in their churches to-day living idle lives, and doing nothing because they did not think they could do anything. In conclusion, the speaker urged the members to make Christ their great example and inspiration,and to follow in His footsteps._</p>
        <p>Mr. OsweLP DICKENS, secretary of the Union, pre- sented his report. He expressed regret that the street collections which were to have been made on behalf of Dr. Barnacle's Hot6es, bad to- be cancelled owing to the inconvenience canted through the change of secretary. They honed, however, to Make a successfnl effort this year,for the glorious cause whose esteemed founder so recently passed away. For thettame reason the :Junior rally was not held, but he was sure a similar disappointment would' not occur this year. The Union, however, bad not been altogether inactive. Thoy had now 13 societies affiliated with the Union, with a total membership of 492. This showed a decrease of three societies,and 90 members, I\cconnted for by the forma- tion of a Union in Newton-in-Makerfield with which the three societies referred te had become affiliated. The societies appeared to have had a successful year, the specittlevork undertaken including the provision of flowers for the sick, raising funds for the erection of a training institute in West Afriea, open-air services daring the summer months, and the distribution of pro- visions to the poor.Theendeavaners of one society had conducted the service in -the absence of the minister 'at one church and arrangements had now been made for them to render this assistance quarterly. Mr. Dickens reviewed the meetings which had been held and urged the importance of a better . attendance at executive committee meetings. For the last twelve months the average attendance at these meetings had been 13 out of 35 which was very, poor indeed. Mr-Dickens appealed to the corresponding secretaries ,to keep hire well supplied with informetion'as to their societies and con- cluded by expressing the wish that the great organiza- tion to which the, belonged might see better and more efileient service in the Warrington district than everf before,and that fill tlie member by consecrated individual f lives might effect much good /for Christ and the i Church. • • . 1 The Rev, H, J. TAXLOR of'.Liverpool said that for 1 heartiness, hopefulness and thoroughness, Christian Endeavour meetings took the first plece. Christian Endeavour was helpine to BANC meetings from deadness and to make Many religious meetings thoroughly welcome, The Christian Endeavour Movement had attaine.d its 25th anniversary and the. story of those 25 years was one of the most,' beautiful Christian romances Of which they knew—how the movement had spread into all parts of the United States; into nearly all the continents and islands of the sea,into all the Protestant denceninntions of Christendom, how it bad taught the youth of the Christian world to link hands all round the globe. There were united in the movement no • less than live millions of people. There was notYns more remereable than that God had raised up this movement of you've revle, and it was His rebuke to Christian pessimists. It was an exceeding great teeny of young wee and maidens raised up with the distinctive mission of ringing the death knell of modern materialism, of division, unholy rivalry and strife and helping to ring 'in the age of spirituality and brotherly co-operatiod for which . the nations had waited too long. They could not attach too much importance to the Movement and, they could hardly expect too much of it. If they were true to themselves and to their ideals God might through that movement bring in the golden age of the world. If they could bring about the time when men</p>
        <p>would be perfectly loyal to Jesus Christ they I would bring about all they wanted. Loyalty to. Christ would win all the elections for Him. It would putito flight the armies of the aliens, and it would make men in character and service all He wanted. It would build up and sanctify all Christian recreations; it would redeem all sports from brutality and wrong doing, and it would sanctify business and domestic life. It would consecrate politics, enthrone conscience,secure good laws, end the reign of bastard imperialism 1 and ring in a true imperialism that cared for men more than for territories and for Jesus Christ. pore than all things of the world. The speaker appealed to his hearers to devote their energies to the great increase campaign which had been started. Increased. Christian endeavour, he pointed out, would mean increased hostility to the drink traffic and increased opposition to militarism. It would not allow Universities to endow military schools and chairs, and it would not encourage Lord Roberts in his mission to teach England to shoot. The disciples of Christ had not been called into being that they might shoot or learn to shoot their fellows. He was glad they had in Christian Endeavour what they called a brotherhood movement, a peace movement which aimed at linking in fellowship the men and women who everywhere stood against militarism, and strove to encourage the youth of the race to refuse to serve in armies. It was their b isiness not to destroy, but to save human life. If they could increase Christian Endeavour they would also build up a strong bulwark against the growth of Roman Catholicism. They must not forget that the Roman Catholics were making desperate efforts. They had increased their priesthood, churches and religious houses phenomenally within recent years on these shores, and they had even put their religion on the rates. There was not a a single Christie Endeavour Society in the Roman Catholic Church or in the High Churches of this land, because Christian Endeavour was Protestant—that was to say it stood for New Testament religion. It stood for the doctrine that every man was saved by grace through faith and by personal contact with Jesus Christ without the intervention of priest of parish or Pope of Rome. They gloried in the Christian Endeavour movement because it made no place for the priest and it was Protestant. They wanted it to increase throughout the world to be a bulwark of the Protestantism in which they all gloried. Miss Woods, of Earlestown, effectively sang a solo. The Convention closed with a consecration meeting conducted by the Rev. H. Wall. Mr. F. Atkinson presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
