      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING Club. Annual Meeting PROPOSED FANCY DRESS PARADE. The Warrington Cycling Club has reached a high level of prosperity and a gratifying record of progress was presented at the annual meeting held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday evening. Mr. D. Hodge prosided over a large attendance which included the Hon. Secretary (Mr. E. Pickavance.) SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING Club. Annual Meeting PROPOSED FANCY DRESS PARADE. The Warrington Cycling Club has reached a high level of prosperity and a gratifying record of progress was presented at the annual meeting held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday evening. Mr. D. Hodge pro- sided over a large attendance which included the Hon. Secretary (Mr. E. Pickavance.) SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>In his annual report Mr. E. PICKAVANCE recorded another successful season. "The weather "he said "has been all that could bo desired and the runs from spring to autumn have been carried out under conditions that would be a credit to many so called summer climes. We can recall a delightful series of pleasant jaunts through fairest scenes of Nature, of exhilarating spins over the brown,rolling moorland, of the indolent loiter- logs through shady avenues redolent with the scent of hawthorn, wild rose and honey suckle, of charming picnics, of heathery slopes formed in settings of dark- hued forest; and lovely rides through the peaceful villages of dear old Cheshire—an unbroken series of pleasurable wanderings that can well compare with any previous season." The runs of the season were des- cribed. On Saturday July Bth the members were again hospitably entertained at Beeston Towers by the Presi- dent (Mr. John Naylor), the fixture being described as one of the most enjoyable in the history of the club. On Saturday September 23rd the annual fishing match was held at Acton Bridge and, 'after great excitement and not too many fish it was found that J. G. Chappell had won the first prize, F. Garnett the second, and G. H. Smith and R. Davies the third and fourth. The ancient game of bowls has been more popular than ever among the metnliers and week after week large numbers turned up at the "Chapel House Sankey. Thirty members competed in the first main the prizes being won by R. Davies, A. Stangle, and G. H. Smith. The most interesting competition, however, was for a silver cup presented by Mr. G. Watkins. Over 40 members entered for this event and after a keen con- test extending over several weeks the cup was won by Mr. F. Redfern. The holiday tours have been greatly enjoyed,Ellesmere being the favourite resort of a considerable section, whilst North Wales, the Lake District and the Lowlands of Scotland claimed a fair share of adherents. The attendance at club runs has averaged 15 for Saturdays and 12 for Tuesday nights. The attendance prizes for Satu, clay and Tuesday night runs combined have been won by Messrs. D. R. Hodge and G. H. Smith,whilst A. Spinks takes the prize for Tuesday night runs only. The social side of the past season has without doubt beE u an unqualified success. The billiard handicap now near- ing completion attracted a record entry. Three first class smokers have been held and have given great pleasure to all who were present. Shortly after the last general meeting the idea was brought up of reviving the old club dances. A com- mittee was formed and a dance was held at the Lion Hotel on March 3rd. The event was a great success and a profit was made of .V 2 ss. '2d. On Thursday, November 23rd another dance was held in the Parr Hall this being also a great success. The membership of the club now stands at 102 active and 11 hon. This is an increase of /9 over last year and proves at once Ow ever increasing popularity 'of the club. The treasurer's balance sheet showed that after meeting all expenses the club have the handsome balance of almost 100 in hand. He wished to take the opportunity of thanking the committee and the members for the kindly support and sympathy which he had at all times received from them during his long tenure of office. Amongst those whom ho desired to especially thank was Miss Kiernan for the free use of the rooms for the many meetings and social gatherings which have been held.</p>
        <p>" It is with feelings of the deepest sorrow" said Mr. Pickavance in conclusion " that we have to record the loss during the. year of Mr. D. Davies ("Pa ") one •of Our oldest and most popular members. His death came as a great shock to the whole Cycling community or the Ncirth. Over sixty members of the club attended the funeral and in addition to a lovely floral tribute from the club many of the members sent similar tokens of respect. Owing to the death of Nit.. Davies the fixture of President's day was abandoned. "In looking backward over the season's proceedings we can claim to have had a very successful '4year and I sincerely hope that proSperity, sociability, and good fellowship will always remain the loading 'features of the Warrington Cycling Club." (Applause.) -FINANCIAL. _ .</p>
        <p>The balance sheet- imbmitted by . Mr. F. GARNETT (treasurpr) was equally gratifying. It showed an income of .£45 ,16s. ad:, and a credit balance of .25 Hs. on the year's working. The total balance . standing to the credit of the club is £29 The Secretary's and 'Treasurer's reports were unani- mously passed. ,Hearty votes of thanks were.accorded to all the retir- ing officers for their services. ELECTION or OFFICERS. • r: John Naylor was unanimously re-elected president of the club, and all the vice-presidents w6i6 re-elected. Mr. E. Pickavance, who has acted as hon. secretary for fifteen years, was re-elected. He pointed out that he was unable .to devoto as much time to the work as formerly, and expressed a desire to have a colleague appointed. No one, however, was willing to undertake the duties, and Mr. Pickavance Wes prevailed upon to aceept office again, seveiPAL members volunteering their willing assistance should occasion arise.----Mr. F. Gar- nett was re-appointod treasurer. Other officers elected were --:--Captain, Mr. G. Bowman 7. sub-captain, ,Mr. F. Fieldhonse • auditors, Messrs. Grounds and Litton ; , committee, Messrs. W. H. Bellian, G. H. Smith, J. L. Davenport, W. Taylor, W. Brown, A. Booth, and D. Hodge. , • , PROPOSED CYCLE PARADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. PiCE"..A,ANCE moved that it he a recommenda- tion to the new committee to consider the organization of .a cycle parade on behalf of the Infirmary extension Lund. The retiring committee, ho said, had,discussed the matter several times and had decided that the time had come when an effort should be made. The Tofirmary was in very extreme need of funds as an extension had become imperative. . Mr. J. L. DAVENPORT seconded., Mr. H. HOPE in supporting said he was a meniber of the Workingmen's Committee of the Infirmary and knew that the extension, was urgently required. During the past twenty years there had heen en increase in the nUmber of cases dealt with irom 3,700 to 10,103 There' had, however, been very little addition to the accommodation of the institution and a big exten- sion was essential. Daring the past three years alone the increase of patients treated had been 3,000. For the E 0 •extension alone 4'10,000 would be required---6,00 for the building and .C 4,000 to acquire and demolish old property. The standing expenditure 'Wnpld of course 1.11, increassd. He was sure the Workingmen's Com- mittee would be willing to render every possible assist- ance to. promoto the success of the parade. The proposition was unanimously agreed to. „</p>
      </div>
