        <p>THE FREE CHURCH - COUNCIL AND SOCIAL 'REFORM. - --- - . , , At a largely attended meeting of the General Com- mittee of the National Council of the Brengel lea! Free Churches, held at tbe Alemo.ial Hall, London, Dr, R. F. Horton,President,in'the chair,a scheme of Social Reform was adopted recording the committee's earnest desire to promote So2;al Reform. Tbe committes expressed Its generat agreement with all efforts to secure : (1 THE JUST TREATMENT 1:11\ CHILDREN. By 1. heprovision of meals either by private sub- scriptions or out of public fund's where ,r.cluirecl for ni -.stiouB childre 2in Public Elementary Eabools. 2. The more carei'ul limitation of Cuild Labour. (b) THE RIGHT TREATMENT OF LABOUR. By 1. Attsmpts to discover the c Luses r-id remedies of unemployment. 1 1 2. One Day's 1:: i: in seven and better regulation where necessary of the hours of labour. 3. The Abolition of Sweating. 4. The Right of Combination and of peaceful persuasion for all Workers and the right of workmen fresly to dispose of labour. 5. Conciliation and Arbitration in Labour Disputc -. G., The Amendment of the Fac,tryAotrs the far it r is necessary, to secure justice , em- ployed and safe and healthy conditions sfo every worker, and to prevent the overwork of women and children. 7. %Improved methods for dealing with the Aged Poor and Infirm. . (c)3 Th THE BETIER HOUSIAG OF THE,WORKING CLASSES. By L e improvement of the law enabling LocA Authorities or the State to provide healthy Idwellings to be let at a fair rent. 2. The strengthening of the law old the improve- ment of administration so as mote effectively . to secure the pnblie health.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. " ITTIAT 15 your Inca or a pneDomenou r nomenon is a clerk who doesn't drop his pen the minute the clock strikes six." JOHNNIE'S MOTHER: "Johnnie, how many times have I told you to stop that noise ?" Johnny (re- flectively): "Seven, mother." A YOUNG man is never so much surprised as when he learns that other young men think his sister is good-looking. PASSENGER: "This is a very slow train, guard." Guard: "Yes, sir I think its them sleeping carriages behind." A FRENCHmAN intending to compliment a young lady by calling her a gentle lamb, said, "She is one tame mutton as is small." OLD FRIEND "Hallos.! So you are in business. now, eh ? Why did you retire from literature?' Be Writer: "Got hungry."• PARLIAMENTARY CANDIDATE (explaining away his defeat): "Yes, gentlemen, I have been defeated; but how have I been defeated ?" Voice in the Crowd: "You dicrt get enough votes." SHE: "This is too much! I won't bear it any longer! To-morrow I shall return to my mother!' He: "My dear—is that a threat—or a promise ?" VISITOR: "Harry said a good thing last night.' Marie: "What was it ?" Visitor: ." He said he had to go early." "YES," he said, " when I was young I was eagerly sought after." "What reward was offered ?" asked the sweet young girl. MAGGIE (to her stepfather, who is very popular with the children): "Oh, I wish you had been here when our papa was- alive. You would have liked each other so much." MR. MANN: "Where in the world can that collar- stud be ?" His Little Daughter: "Have you looked where you'd never think of finding it. papa?" HIBERNATING IlAwiiirs: " What s der matter, Bill? Yer restless !" Wobbling William: " Yes ! I don't sleep good I must have insomnia. I wake up every two or t'ree days!" IN front of a grocer's in the Rue de Rivoli, Paris. is a sign which reads, Madeira, two francs; old Madeira, three francs; genuine Madeira, 10 francs." ANGRY MOTHER: "Now, Bobby, don't let me speak to you again." Bobby (helplessly): "How can I prevent you, mamma ?' "My hair," mused Van Smythe, as he carefully consigned his two remaining wisps to their appointed places, "reminds me most painfully of a fool and his money." THE MARRIED ONE: "Can you imagine anything worse than marriage without love ?" The Unmarried One: "Yes, I think I can. Love without marriage, for instance." Ricims may on the whole be hurtful to the man who possesses them, but we still meet with men of weak finances and iron constitutions who are ready and willing to undertake all risks. " NEHEMIAH, compare the abjective cold," said a schoolmaster to his head boy. "Positive cold, com- parative cough, superlative coffin," triumphantly re- sponded Nehemiah. "MARY, Johnny tells me that when he went into the dining-room last night he saw Mr. Biff with his arm round your waist." "What a story, mamma! Why, the gas was out." A. SHOPKEEPER had for his virtues obtained the name of the little rascal." A stranger asked hint why this appellation had been given to him. "To distinguish me front the rest of my trade," quoth he, ". who are all great rascals." . MAY IA : "Just look at the front of your new coat; I don't think it is the slightest, use to try to keep you clean." Johnny (eagerly) : "Ain't you going to try any more? WHAT are you after, my dear ?" said a grand- mother to a little boy, who was sliding along a room, and casting furtive glances at a gentleman who was paying a visit. "I am trying. grandma, to steal papa's hat out of the room without, letting the gentleman see it : he wants him to think he's out." MAMMA (sternly): "Don't you know that the great Ring Solomon said, Spare the rod and spoil the child'?" Bobby: " Yes ; but he didn't say that until he was growed upY RAGGED RICHARD (insinuatingly): " I say, mister, have yer got any suggestions ter make a feller wa'at ain't able ter raise er penny ter git shaved with :0" Grumble (passing on): " Yes ; grow a beard." IRATE FATHER: "I wonder what makes my razor so dull ?" Angel-Child: "Dull, papa? Why, it was beautiful and sharp when I made my boat with it yesterday." Miss : "So you have been married a year. Has your husband found out about your dyed hair, false teeth, or glass eye yet ?" Mrs. Wedlate : "No, indeed; he's been too busy concealing the same defects in himself." ETHEL : "Why, what's the matter, Gertrude ?" Gertrude: "Oh, nothing. Only Jack and I had a quarrel the other day, and I wrote and told him never to dare speak or write to me again—and the wretch hasn't even had the decency to answer my letter." "I WOULDN'T have refused that young man if I'd been you,' said a maiden aunt to her young and frisky niece. " 1 don't think I would either, if l'd been you," retorted the saucy maiden. "1 itAvE invented a new chair for the theatre." " Well, what is its particular merit ?" Oh, you just touch a button, and it sinks through the floor to the smoking-room below." "For whom is it in- tended ?" " The fellow who leaves his seat in the middle of a row at the end of each act." CREDITOR (determinedly): " I shall call at your hoitse every week until you pay this account, sir." Debtor (in ;the blandest of tones): 'Then, sir, there seems every probability of our acquaintanceship ripening into friendship.' MR. COURTNEY (flatteringly): "I had the blues awfully when I came here to-night, Miss Fisher. but they are all gone now. You are as good as medicine." Miss Fisher s Little Brother: " Yes ; father himself says she'll be a drug in the market if she doesn't catch on to some fellow soon." MornEit : " And so your friend Clara is soon to be married ?" Daughter (just returned from long absence) : " Yes ; doesn't it seem strange? I hadn't heard a word about it until 1 called to see her this morning. She showed me her trousseau. It's per- fectly lovely, just from Paris, and she has the hand- somest ring I ever saw, and she showed me the house she is to live in, and the furniture she has selected, and the horses and carriages she is to have. She showed me everything except the man she is to marry. I think she forgot about him." AT the country house of a certain popular baronet some time ago the butler came into his master's room early one morning, wearing a most woe-begone ex- pression. "There's very bad news for you this morning," he said. "What is it, John ?" inquired the baronet. "Poor Air. ," mentioning the name of a gentleman in an adjacent village, "has been an gone an' hung himself. An' that isn't the worst, by any means, Sir William; that there fox has been into the preserves again and killed twenty of the young pheasants !" "THEY say Greatbrane's new tragedy received an ovation on its first presentation. Is it a fact?' "Yes. Couldn't very well have been otherwise.' " How's that ?" "He gave free tickets to every undertaker in town, and they were all there. As they sat and saw men, women, and children killed by the score in each act, their professional instinct caused their enthusiasm to become boundless, and their applause almost lifted the roof off. Greatbane is all right if his traredv isn't."</p>
