        <p>"Y" Branch B.W.T.A. RUNCORN AND WIDNES. An open meeting of the above Society was held in Friar's Gate House on Thursday evening last week, and was well attended. Mr. Tilling presided, and the CHILD DROWNED AT WIDNES. following programme was gone through :—Chairman's On Monday afternoon Leonard Cornbes, aged three son of William Combes, a labourer, of 39 address; recitation, Mrs. Tanner ; piano duet, Miss years,,aklid-street, was drowned in the Mersey at, Widnes Fitchett and Miss Dale • recitation, Miss A. Taylor He was playing on some rocks with several other boys, ' dialogue, " Wanted—a Cook," by seven members of when he slipped and fell into the water and was carried the Workingmen's Mission. away by the strong tide which was running. PIIM PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST ATTEMPTED FRAUD AND SECRET BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. COMMISSIONS. Ancient and Modern Humour. , On Monday, at Widnes, Patrick Burns, a stranger There was a crowded attrndance of the members of was charged on remand with having attempted to obtain the Warrington Society at the Old Academy ouThurs- money by false pretences. It was alleged that the day evening when a paper was presented by Mr. H. IL prAreissosrnse.r, uondssaegre,sthsecappwreotrekxst, worfotbeetiong a thefcnorinllncsemaarneeant Ashton on "Some samples and comparisons of Colliery Company asking for some allowance for prais- ancient and modern humour." Mr. F. R. Roberts in g and supporting their firestuff. He requested that an presided. Mr. Ashton in his paper took as an answer should be sent to the Post Office. It was found exponent of ancient hrunour Aristophanes, the Greek, that the prisoner had never worked at Gossage's works. contrasting his work with that of Barry Pain, —He pleaded guilty, and was remanded for a week. Thackeray, and Mark Twain. He came to the con- I BREAKING INTO A BOOKING OFFICE. elusion that the ancients had very little humour, this Two boys named Henry Rimmer (13) and James being largely the invention of modern times. A Rimmer (11) were charged' at Widnes on Monday with lively discussion ensued in which Mr. Lofthouse, Mr. breaking and entering the booking office at Ditton Madeley, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook and Alderman Railway Station and stealing 3s. Gd. On Friday morn- Bennett took part. Mr. Ashton was heartily thanked ing the booking clerk left the office, and on returning a for his paper. I few minutes later he found that 3s. Gd. was missing. roof."W h When Detectivearrested HenryG o said,Good said"tlhelipfltacede hJaadmebseenohentotetrhede Iron Fencing of every description. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Iby means of the ventilator. The boy James could not Warrington.—Advt. be questioned as he was too deaf, and in consequence of this the charge against him was withdrawn,the police ' undertaking to send him to a deaf and dumb school. The elder boy was bound over. RUNCORN'S NEW WATER SUPPLY. At noon, on Monday, Alderman Burgess, chairman of the Liverpool Corporation Waterworks Committee, per- formed the ceremony of turning Vyrnwy water into Runcorn reservoir. Connections have been made with the Vyrnwy main at Norton Tower at a cost of over £6,000, and Runcorn Council are under an agreement to take 300,000 gallons a day at Gd. per 1,000 gallons. Runcorn water pumped from red sandstone at great depth is very hard, and contains about forty-five grains of salt to the gallon. The soft Welsh water has. there-. fore, been obtained chiefly in the interests of manu- facturers in Runcorn, Halton, and Weston. A large company witnessed the ceremony. and subsequently Mr Samuel Taylor, chairman of the Urban District Council, entertained them to luncheon at the Town Hall. WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL. The monthly meeting of the Widnes Town Council was held on Tuesday, the Mayor (Alderman S. Quinn) presiding.—Mr. Lewis, in proposing the adoption of the minutes of the Health Committee, referred to the number of deaths,and said a large majority of them had occurred amongst children. Nearly half of them were under one year, and three-fifths of the whole were five years. Mr. Smith made a complaint in regard to the night soil department, and said if at the conclusion of the Council the committee made an inspection, and found his statements to be untrue, he was prepared to give a guinea to any charity.—Mr. Lewis said the matter would be thoroughly investigated.— Alderman! Timrais proposed the adoption of the minutes orthe Gas and Water Cornmittee,and said that , some time ago he had seen where the Liverpool Corpora- tion had purchased 200 acres of land at Allerton for the purpose of a cemetery. At that time it did not strike him as being dangerous to Widnes. His father (Mr. T. Sutton Timmis) had, however, asked him hm, it would affect the water supplies of Widnes at Netherby and Belle Vale' and inconsequence of that he had communi- cated with the Town clerk and the engineer. They discussed the matter, and came to the csmclusion that it was a very important question, and, that they should obtain the very best advice that could be procured. Following upon that two experts had made an inspection of the land, and they would shortly report to the com- mittee.—The minutes were adopteld.—The General Pur- poses Committee reported that letters had been received from congregations of several places of worship, urging upon the Town Council the wisdom of posting up public notices setting forth the mischief caused by indulgence in alcohol, as done in many towns in England and on the Continent. Alderman Gossage said the Town Council had no legal power to spend money for this purpose.</p>
